{"question_id": "Medical-b7f57756", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the context, less common cancer subtypes in bladder cancer include adenocarcinoma and small cell carcinoma.", "contexts": ["pathologist. The pathologist will compare the cancer cells to normal cells. The more different they look, the higher the grade. And the higher the grade, the faster the cancer is expected to spread. LG means that the cancer cells are low grade (slow growing). HG means that the cancer cells are high grade (fast growing). The inner lining of the bladder is made of urothelial cells. Most cases of bladder cancer start in the urothelial cells. So this type of cancer is called urothelial cancer. But in rare cases, urothelial cell cancer has features that look like other types of cells. These are referred to as subtypes. Some of them include: Glandular subtype Squamous cell subtype Micropapillary subtype Plasmacytoid subtype Sarcomatoid subtype Nested subtype Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype Microcystic and tubular subtype It鈥檚 important to investigate whether you have one of these or another subtype. In general, treatment for these uncommon subtypes is similar in the early stages to treatment for typical urothelial cancer. However, some subtypes might grow or advance faster than typical urothelial cell cancer. And faster growth might mean it needs more aggressive treatment. Key points An important thing about bladder cancer is whether it has or it hasn鈥檛 grown into the muscle layer. Cancer that has invaded the muscle needs more aggressive treatment. The stage of cancer describes how deep it has grown into the bladder wall and how far it has spread. Cancer stage is used to determine which tests and treatments will help you receive the best outcome. Bladder cancer at stage 0 and stage 1 haven鈥檛 invaded the muscle layer. Stage 0 means that abnormal cells have grown on the inside lining of the bladder. Stage 1 means that a tumor has formed and has invaded the layer of connective tissue in the bladder wall. Stages 2, 3, and 4 bladder cancers are muscle-invasive. The tumor has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall. In stage 3 bladder cancer, the tumor has grown into the fat layer around the bladder. There may also be cancer in nearby lymph nodes. Stage 4 is metastatic bladder cancer. This is cancer that has spread outside the bladder and to distant sites in the body. Cancer grade is an estimate of how fast the cancer is expected to grow. Low grade bladder cancer is slow growing. High grade cancer grows more quickly. We want your feedback! Our goal is to provide helpful and easy-to-understand information on cancer. Take our survey to let us know what we got right and what we could do better. NCCN.org/patients/feedback Treatment options for bladder cancer Surgery Intravesical therapy Systemic therapy Radiation therapy Clinical trials Supportive care Side effects of treatment Key points What鈥檚 your stage? This chapter explains the different treatment options for bladder cancer. Many people with bladder cancer receive more than one type of treatment. Together, you and your care team will choose a treatment plan that鈥檚 right for you. Treatment for bladder cancer is based on many things. This includes the extent, severity, and type of cancer. It also includes your age, your ability to perform daily tasks, your other health issues, and the availability and affordability of drugs. Importantly, treatment is also based on your wishes and preferences. There are several ways to treat bladder cancer. It鈥檚 likely that you鈥檒l need a few different types of treatment over months or years. Treatments include: Surgery 鈥?The goal of surgery is to take out the tumor and sometimes the surrounding tissue. Surgery procedures range from snipping a single tumor from the bladder lining to removing the entire bladder and other organs. Intravesical therapy 鈥?Intravesical therapy involves filling the bladder with a liquid medication that helps destroy cancer cells. Systemic therapy 鈥?Systemic therapy is typically an intravenous (IV) treatment that treats cancer anywhere in the whole body. Types of systemic therapy include chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy. Radiation therapy 鈥?Radiation therapy is a local therapy, which means it affects a specific area of the body. Sometimes chemotherapy or another therapy is added to radiation therapy to enhance treatment. Clinical trial 鈥?Clinical trials are done to test experimental treatments. You may be able to join a clinical trial at any time. Surgery Types of surgery for bladder cancer include tumor removal and bladder removal. Tumor removal For bladder cancer, a surgical procedure called a resection removes the tumor but leaves the rest of the bladder in place. The full name of the procedure is called a transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT, sometimes pronounced tur-bit). Even though it has a complicated name, the concept of TURBT is simple. It鈥檚 a procedure that removes tumors through the urethra without having to cut into the abdomen. The tumor can then be tested to provide a more detailed diagnosis. TURBT is done in an operating room in a clinic or hospital. You鈥檒l be given anesthesia to put you to sleep. The urologist then guides an instrument with a small cutting device through the urethra and into the bladder. The instrument is used to examine and remove the tumor from the bladder lining. The goals of TURBT are to: Confirm the initial bladder cancer diagnosis. Take a sample of the bladder wall to see if the tumor has invaded the muscle layer. Remove all the visible tumor. After the procedure, most people can go home the same day. But you鈥檒l need a few days to rest and recover. You may feel pain or burning when you urinate and have blood in your urine for the following 2 weeks or so. Another TURBT procedure may be needed about 2 to 6 weeks after the first one. The second TURBT is done to make sure all the tumor is removed. Or it may be done to get a deeper biopsy sample for the pathologist to examine. A treatment called intravesical therapy is often done in combination with TURBT."], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Mucinous Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Adenocarcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Adenocarcinoma is a broad category of cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are responsible for producing and secreting substances such as mucus. This cancer type can arise in various organs, and its characteristics and prevalence differ depending on the location. In the lungs, adenocarcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) that begins in the mucus-making cells lining the alveoli. It is the most common subtype of NSCLC. In the colorectal region, adenocarcinoma is the most frequent type of tumor, forming within adenomas (precancerous polyps) and representing the most common form of both colon and rectal cancer. It also accounts for the vast majority of colorectal cancers overall, distinguishing it from cancers arising from squamous cells.\n\nAdenocarcinoma also occurs in other parts of the digestive and reproductive systems. In the stomach, it is the most common type of cancer, developing from the mucus-secreting cells that line the stomach wall; indeed, nearly all stomach cancers are adenocarcinomas. In the cervix, adenocarcinoma is a recognized type of cervical cancer that arises from the glandular cells located in the endocervix, and it is considered a common form of this disease. Additionally, adenocarcinoma can appear in the bladder, where it is a less common, non-urothelial type of cancer. Across all these sites, the defining feature of adenocarcinoma is its origin in glandular tissue, which influences its behavior, diagnosis, and treatment.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682927}, {"entity_name": "Low-Grade Serous Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer with low-grade features.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the bladder, most commonly originating in the urothelial cells that line the inside of the organ. This abnormal cell growth can be classified into several clinical categories, including non-muscle-invasive, muscle-invasive, and metastatic forms, reflecting the extent to which the cancer has spread within or beyond the bladder wall. The disease is most frequently diagnosed in adults aged 55 and older, and its development is influenced by risk factors such as smoking and genetic predisposition.\n\nDiagnosis of bladder cancer typically involves a range of tests and procedures aimed at detecting the presence and extent of the malignancy. The condition is often identified through imaging, urine tests, and biopsy, with the specific diagnostic approach tailored to the individual case. Once diagnosed, the cancer is staged and graded to determine its aggressiveness and spread, which directly informs the treatment strategy.\n\nTreatment for bladder cancer is highly individualized, depending on the stage and grade of the tumor, the patient鈥檚 overall health, and their personal preferences. Options may include surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, immunotherapy, or a combination of these approaches. Given the complexity of the disease, management often requires a multidisciplinary care team, including urologists, oncologists, and other specialists, to coordinate comprehensive care. The goal of treatment is to remove or destroy the cancer while preserving bladder function whenever possible, though more advanced or recurrent cases may necessitate more aggressive interventions.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"entity_name": "Malignant Germ Cell Tumors", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Less common non-epithelial ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Small Cell Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common type of bladder cancer.<SEP>A non-urothelial type of bladder cancer that is less common.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Clear Cell Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Tumor", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor is an abnormal mass or growth of tissue that arises from the uncontrolled overgrowth of cells, and it can be either benign (non-cancerous) or malignant (cancerous). In the context of cancer, a tumor specifically refers to the primary cancerous mass, which is a key element in staging and treatment planning. Tumors can develop in various parts of the body, including lymph nodes, the prostate, the bladder, bones, the throat, and the ovaries, where they may present as solid or fluid-filled masses. Their size is often measured in centimeters or millimeters, and this measurement, along with the extent of local invasion into nearby structures, is used to assign a T score in cancer staging systems. For example, primary bone tumors are staged based on size and location, while throat and bladder tumors are assessed for their potential to invade deeper tissue layers or spread. Tumors may be detected as irregular or hardened areas during physical examination, such as in the prostate, or identified through medical procedures. Biopsy samples are commonly taken from tumors to determine whether they are cancerous, and in many cases, tumors can be surgically removed. Overall, the term tumor encompasses a wide range of abnormal growths, with its clinical significance largely determined by its benign or malignant nature, size, location, and potential for invasion or metastasis.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682948}, {"entity_name": "Low-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer with low-grade features.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Kidney Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A disease characterized by malignant cells in the kidneys.<SEP>Cancer that starts in the tissues of the kidneys, with most being carcinomas.<SEP>Cancer that arises in the kidney, with renal cell carcinoma being the most common type.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683065}, {"entity_name": "RCC Subtypes", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Different subtypes of renal cell carcinoma, a type of kidney cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683065}, {"entity_name": "Malignant Sex-Cord Stromal Tumors", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Less common non-epithelial ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Low Grade Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer that is slow growing, indicated by LG.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Inhibin", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor marker (typically inhibin A and B) found in higher amounts in less common ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683054}, {"entity_name": "Urothelial Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The most common type of bladder cancer, arising from urothelial cells.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "CA 19-9", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683054}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "White Males", "description": "White males have a lower risk of prostate cancer compared to Black males.<SEP>White males have a lower risk of developing prostate cancer compared to Black males.", "keywords": "incidence,lower risk,risk", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Adenocarcinoma", "tgt_id": "Bladder Cancer", "description": "Adenocarcinoma is a type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "classification,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Exercise", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Exercise may decrease the aggressiveness of prostate cancer and the likelihood of dying from it.", "keywords": "protective factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Small Cell Carcinoma", "description": "Small cell carcinoma is a type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "classification,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Low-Risk Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Low-risk prostate cancer is a category indicating a lower chance of recurrence or spread.", "keywords": "prognosis,risk classification", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Tumor", "description": "Bladder cancer may form a tumor in the bladder.", "keywords": "pathology,progression", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Mucinous Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Mucinous carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Kidney Cancer", "tgt_id": "RCC Subtypes", "description": "RCC subtypes are different types of renal cell carcinoma, a form of kidney cancer.", "keywords": "classification,types", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Low-Grade Serous Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Low-grade serous carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Low Grade Bladder Cancer", "description": "Low grade bladder cancer is a slow-growing form of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "grade", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Malignant Germ Cell Tumors", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Malignant germ cell tumors are a less common non-epithelial subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Urothelial Cancer", "description": "Urothelial cancer is the most common type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Clear Cell Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Clear cell carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Breast Cancer", "tgt_id": "Carcinoma", "description": "Almost all breast cancers are a subtype of carcinoma.<SEP>Almost all breast cancers are carcinomas.", "keywords": "cancer,disease,subtype of,type of", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Low-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Low-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Pancreatic Adenocarcinomas", "tgt_id": "Pancreatic Cancer", "description": "Pancreatic adenocarcinomas are a type of pancreatic cancer.", "keywords": "subtype,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Malignant Sex-Cord Stromal Tumors", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Malignant sex-cord stromal tumors are a less common non-epithelial subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Urothelium", "description": "Bladder cancer often starts in the urothelium.<SEP>Bladder cancer often starts in the urothelial cells lining the inside of the bladder.", "keywords": "cancer type,disease,originates in,tissue", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Inhibin", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Inhibin is a tumor marker found in higher amounts in less common ovarian cancers.", "keywords": "less common,tumor marker", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Cancer Subtype", "tgt_id": "Patient", "description": "The cancer subtype is considered when choosing treatment.", "keywords": "consideration,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "CA 19-9", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "CA 19-9 is a tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "keywords": "less common,tumor marker", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage", "description": "Bladder cancer is classified into stages (0-4) based on how far it has grown into the bladder wall and spread.", "keywords": "cancer progression,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Borderline Epithelial Tumor", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Borderline epithelial tumor is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "High Grade Bladder Cancer", "description": "High grade bladder cancer is a fast-growing form of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "grade", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "HE4", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "HE4 is a tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "keywords": "less common,tumor marker", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Cancer Stage", "description": "Cancer stage is used to classify bladder cancer and guide treatment.", "keywords": "classification", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Carcinosarcoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Carcinosarcoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Connective Tissue Layer", "tgt_id": "Stage 1", "description": "Stage 1 bladder cancer has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue layer.", "keywords": "cancer invasion,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP)", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "AFP is a tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "keywords": "less common,tumor marker", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Urothelial Carcinoma", "description": "Urothelial carcinoma shares features with bladder cancer, and more information is available in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer.", "keywords": "information,related to", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Beta-Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (尾-hCG)", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "尾-hCG is a tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "keywords": "less common,tumor marker", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Bladder Cancer", "description": "Metastatic bladder cancer is a phase of bladder cancer where the cancer has spread to other parts of the body.<SEP>Metastatic bladder cancer is an advanced stage of bladder cancer that has spread.", "keywords": "phase,progression,spread,stage", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bleeding or Bruising Easily", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Bleeding or bruising easily is a common side effect of chemotherapy.", "keywords": "side effect of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "description": "Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "classification,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5740, "output_tokens": 48, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 7741.331700002775, "generation_time_ms": 1020.3185999998823, "total_time_ms": 8762.12279999163, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["cancer subtypes", "bladder cancer"], "low_level": ["less common"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 88, "total_relations_found": 89, "entities_after_truncation": 16, "relations_after_truncation": 34, "merged_chunks_count": 48, "final_chunks_count": 1}}, "question": "Which cancer subtypes are less common in bladder cancer?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and small cell carcinoma are less common.", "evidence": "Squamous cell carcinoma is a less common subtype of bladder cancer.; Adenocarcinoma is a less common subtype of bladder cancer.; Small cell carcinoma is a less common subtype of bladder cancer.", "evidence_relations": "Squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and small cell carcinoma are less common subtypes of bladder cancer", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-0535a6b1", "method": "lightrag", "question": "What tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the bile duct and gallbladder?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer.", "evidence": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the bile duct.; Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the gallbladder.", "split": "train", "error": "RuntimeError: Backend returned an empty answer or no contexts"}
{"question_id": "Medical-0535a6b1", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the gallbladder and bile ducts.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Muscle\", \"entity_type\": \"biologicaltissue\", \"description\": \"Tissue found between the membranes of the bile duct.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Muscle Layer\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"A layer of soft tissue that helps move food through the stomach, also called muscularis propria.<SEP>A layer of the stomach wall, part of the five main layers.<SEP>The layer of the bladder wall that muscle-invasive cancer has invaded.<SEP>The layer of the bladder wall that cancer must invade to be considered muscle-invasive.<SEP>The muscular layer of the bladder wall; invasion into this layer determines treatment aggressiveness.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Muscle (Muscularis)\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of soft tissue in the walls of the gallbladder and bile ducts.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stomach Wall\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The anatomical structure of the stomach composed of five layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscle, subserosa, and serosa.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Hepatobiliary System\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The system comprising the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, involved in bile production and part of the digestive system.<SEP>The system comprising the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stomach\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"Organ where food passes from the esophagus and is broken down.<SEP>Organ that meets the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction, containing acid.<SEP>An organ near the liver.<SEP>A muscular, hollow organ that secretes enzymes and acid to aid digestion.<SEP>A digestive organ that receives food from the esophagus, mixes it with enzymes and acid, and releases chyme into the small intestine. It has parts including cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682946}, {\"entity_name\": \"Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of connective tissue that surrounds muscle in the gallbladder and bile ducts.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Subserosa\", \"entity_type\": \"artifact\", \"description\": \"A layer of connective tissue supporting the serosa.<SEP>A layer of the stomach wall, part of the five main layers.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Gallbladder\", \"entity_type\": \"artifact\", \"description\": \"An organ that stores bile, with layers including epithelium, lamina propria, muscle, perimuscular fibrous tissue, and serosa.<SEP>A small, pear-shaped organ under the liver that stores bile.<SEP>An organ in the digestive system.<SEP>An organ that stores bile produced by the liver.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Breast\", \"entity_type\": \"naturalobject\", \"description\": \"An organ and gland on the chest, composed of milk ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>An organ and gland on the chest made of milk ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>The organ on the chest that contains lobules, ducts, fat, and other tissues.<SEP>An organ and gland on the chest made of milk ducts, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>The organ in the chest that produces milk, consisting of lobules and ducts.<SEP>Organ in the chest, site of breast cancer, often imaged and biopsied.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683079}, {\"entity_name\": \"Bile Duct\", \"entity_type\": \"artifact\", \"description\": \"A tube that carries bile, made up of several layers including epithelium, lamina propria, muscle, perimuscular fibrous tissue, and serosa.<SEP>A duct made up of several layers including inner and outer membranes, with muscle and connective tissue in between.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Connective Tissue\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Connective Tissue is a fundamental biological material that serves to support, connect, and surround other tissues and structures within the body. It is found in a wide variety of anatomical locations, including the layers of the gallbladder, bile ducts, stomach wall, and bladder wall. In the context of the bladder, it is the specific layer of tissue that stage 1 cancer invades. Within the breast, connective tissue acts as a supporting framework that holds structures together, surrounds other components, and includes ligaments. Additionally, it is the tissue obtained from a skin punch biopsy, which is used to extract DNA for analysis. In pathology, connective tissue can also be fibrous tissue that some tumors form, and its presence in a tumor is an indicator of a high-risk or very high-risk cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682895}, {\"entity_name\": \"Pelvic Floor\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A group of muscles supporting pelvic organs, involved in bowel, bladder, and sexual function.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-195\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683089}, {\"entity_name\": \"Serosa\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"An outer membrane that covers the gallbladder and bile ducts, also called the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum.<SEP>A membrane also called the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, which is a thin layer covering a surface.<SEP>The outer layer of the colon wall, also called adventitia.<SEP>The outer membrane covering the stomach, allowing smooth movement against other organs.<SEP>The outermost layer of the stomach wall.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Dermis\", \"entity_type\": \"naturalobject\", \"description\": \"The middle layer of skin, containing connective tissue, blood vessels, oil and sweat glands, nerves, and hair follicles.<SEP>The middle layer of skin containing connective tissue, hair follicles, and sweat glands.<SEP>The layer of skin beneath the epidermis, partially removed during a superficial shave biopsy.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682760}, {\"entity_name\": \"Membrane\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A very thin layer that covers a surface, such as the inner and outer layers of the bile duct.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Skin\", \"entity_type\": \"naturalobject\", \"description\": \"The body's largest organ, acting as a barrier, with three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.<SEP>The largest organ of the body, protecting against invaders, controlling body temperature, and allowing sensations. It has three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.<SEP>Tissue not affected by leukemia, used to test for predisposition syndromes.<SEP>A site where leukemia can spread outside the bloodstream.<SEP>The outer covering of the body, a site for HPV infection.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682760}, {\"entity_name\": \"Bladder Wall\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"The muscular wall of the bladder that can be invaded by cancer.<SEP>The wall of the bladder, composed of four main layers: inner lining (urothelial layer), connective tissue layer, muscle layer, and outer fatty layer.<SEP>The wall of the bladder, composed of layers including the lining, connective tissue, muscle, and fat.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683089}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Bladder\", \"tgt_id\": \"Bladder Cancer\", \"description\": \"Bladder cancer occurs in the bladder.<SEP>Bladder cancer originates in the bladder.<SEP>Bladder cancer is located in the bladder, which is examined during cystoscopy.\", \"keywords\": \"disease,located in,location,organ,origin\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Muscle Layer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach Wall\", \"description\": \"The muscle layer is one of the five layers of the stomach wall.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,layers\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer\", \"description\": \"Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a phase of bladder cancer where the tumor has invaded the muscle layer.<SEP>Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a more advanced stage of bladder cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"phase,progression,stage\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Muscle Layer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach\", \"description\": \"The stomach wall has a muscle layer.\", \"keywords\": \"has layer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer\", \"description\": \"Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a phase of bladder cancer where the cancer has not invaded the muscle layer.\", \"keywords\": \"phase,progression\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Muscle Layer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Subserosa\", \"description\": \"The muscle layer is surrounded by the subserosa.\", \"keywords\": \"adjacent to\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 2 Bladder Cancer\", \"description\": \"Stage 2 bladder cancer is muscle-invasive.\", \"keywords\": \"stage\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Connective Tissue\", \"description\": \"The breast contains connective tissue.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,part of,support\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Connective Tissue\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"The presence of connective tissue in a tumor is a sign of high-risk or very high-risk squamous cell skin cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"indicator,risk\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Serosa\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach Wall\", \"description\": \"The serosa is the outermost layer of the stomach wall.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,layers\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Breast Cancer\", \"description\": \"Breast cancer starts in the cells of the breast.<SEP>Breast cancer starts in the cells of the breast, often in the ducts or lobules.\", \"keywords\": \"affects,disease,originates in,starts in\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Dermis\", \"tgt_id\": \"Skin\", \"description\": \"The skin has a middle layer called the dermis.\", \"keywords\": \"has layer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683096}, {\"src_id\": \"Gallbladder\", \"tgt_id\": \"Liver\", \"description\": \"The gallbladder is located near the liver.\", \"keywords\": \"near,related\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Epithelium\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovaries\", \"description\": \"The epithelium is the surface layer of tissue surrounding the ovaries, where most ovarian cancers start.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,cancer origin\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Hepatobiliary System\", \"tgt_id\": \"Liver\", \"description\": \"The hepatobiliary system includes the liver.<SEP>The liver is part of the hepatobiliary system.\", \"keywords\": \"includes,part of\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Connective Tissue Layer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 1\", \"description\": \"Stage 1 bladder cancer has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue layer.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer invasion,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Bile\", \"tgt_id\": \"Liver\", \"description\": \"Liver cells make bile.<SEP>The liver makes bile.\", \"keywords\": \"makes,produces\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Gerota's Fascia\", \"tgt_id\": \"Kidneys\", \"description\": \"The kidney and adrenal gland are surrounded by a layer of fat, covered by Gerota's fascia.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,surrounded by\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Hepatic Ducts\", \"tgt_id\": \"Liver\", \"description\": \"Bile flows into small channels that drain into ducts, which form the hepatic ducts.\", \"keywords\": \"drains into\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Pathologist\", \"tgt_id\": \"Tumor Type\", \"description\": \"A pathologist analyzes tissue samples to determine the specific type and subtype of squamous cell skin cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"analysis,diagnosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Vessels\", \"description\": \"The breast contains lymph vessels.<SEP>The breast contains lymph vessels that carry lymph fluid.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,lymphatic system\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder Wall\", \"tgt_id\": \"Connective Tissue\", \"description\": \"Connective tissue is a layer of the bladder wall.\", \"keywords\": \"part of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder\", \"tgt_id\": \"PET Scan\", \"description\": \"The bladder normally appears bright on PET scans.\", \"keywords\": \"imaging,normal uptake\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Muscle (Muscularis)\", \"tgt_id\": \"Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue\", \"description\": \"Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer.\", \"keywords\": \"surrounds\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Glands\", \"tgt_id\": \"Head And Neck Cancer\", \"description\": \"Head and neck cancer may cause symptoms in the glands.\", \"keywords\": \"symptoms in\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Stomach Wall\", \"tgt_id\": \"Submucosa\", \"description\": \"The submucosa is the second layer of the stomach wall.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,layers\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ducts\", \"description\": \"The breast contains ducts that connect lobules to the nipple.<SEP>The breast contains ducts.<SEP>The breast contains ducts that carry milk from the lobules to the nipple.<SEP>The breast contains milk ducts.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,milk transport,part of\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Epidermis\", \"tgt_id\": \"Skin\", \"description\": \"The skin has an outermost layer called the epidermis.\", \"keywords\": \"has layer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683096}, {\"src_id\": \"Esophagus\", \"tgt_id\": \"Submucosa\", \"description\": \"The submucosa is a layer of connective tissue below the mucosa in the esophagus.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,layer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lobules\", \"description\": \"The breast contains lobules, which are milk glands.<SEP>The breast contains lobules.<SEP>The breast contains lobules, which are glands that can make milk.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,milk production,part of\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Kidneys\", \"tgt_id\": \"Renal Capsule\", \"description\": \"The kidney is covered by a thin layer of tissue called the renal capsule.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,covered by\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Blood Vessels\", \"tgt_id\": \"Breast\", \"description\": \"The breast contains blood vessels.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,part of,vascular supply\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Hypodermis\", \"tgt_id\": \"Skin\", \"description\": \"The skin has a deepest layer called the hypodermis.\", \"keywords\": \"has layer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683096}, {\"src_id\": \"Axillary Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Breast\", \"description\": \"Lymph from breast tissue drains into axillary lymph nodes.<SEP>Lymph fluid from breast tissue drains into axillary lymph nodes.<SEP>Lymph from breast tissue drains into lymph vessels and travels to axillary lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer spread,drains to,lymphatic drainage\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast\", \"tgt_id\": \"Fat\", \"description\": \"The breast contains fat.<SEP>The breast contains fat tissue.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,contains,part of\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Axillary Lymph Node\", \"tgt_id\": \"Breast\", \"description\": \"Lymph from breast tissue drains to axillary lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,drains to\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Muscle", "entity_type": "biologicaltissue", "description": "Tissue found between the membranes of the bile duct.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Muscle Layer", "entity_type": "location", "description": "A layer of soft tissue that helps move food through the stomach, also called muscularis propria.<SEP>A layer of the stomach wall, part of the five main layers.<SEP>The layer of the bladder wall that muscle-invasive cancer has invaded.<SEP>The layer of the bladder wall that cancer must invade to be considered muscle-invasive.<SEP>The muscular layer of the bladder wall; invasion into this layer determines treatment aggressiveness.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Muscle (Muscularis)", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of soft tissue in the walls of the gallbladder and bile ducts.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Stomach Wall", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The anatomical structure of the stomach composed of five layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscle, subserosa, and serosa.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Hepatobiliary System", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The system comprising the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, involved in bile production and part of the digestive system.<SEP>The system comprising the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Stomach", "entity_type": "location", "description": "Organ where food passes from the esophagus and is broken down.<SEP>Organ that meets the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction, containing acid.<SEP>An organ near the liver.<SEP>A muscular, hollow organ that secretes enzymes and acid to aid digestion.<SEP>A digestive organ that receives food from the esophagus, mixes it with enzymes and acid, and releases chyme into the small intestine. It has parts including cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682946}, {"entity_name": "Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of connective tissue that surrounds muscle in the gallbladder and bile ducts.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Subserosa", "entity_type": "artifact", "description": "A layer of connective tissue supporting the serosa.<SEP>A layer of the stomach wall, part of the five main layers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Gallbladder", "entity_type": "artifact", "description": "An organ that stores bile, with layers including epithelium, lamina propria, muscle, perimuscular fibrous tissue, and serosa.<SEP>A small, pear-shaped organ under the liver that stores bile.<SEP>An organ in the digestive system.<SEP>An organ that stores bile produced by the liver.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Breast", "entity_type": "naturalobject", "description": "An organ and gland on the chest, composed of milk ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>An organ and gland on the chest made of milk ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>The organ on the chest that contains lobules, ducts, fat, and other tissues.<SEP>An organ and gland on the chest made of milk ducts, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, ligaments, and connective tissue.<SEP>The organ in the chest that produces milk, consisting of lobules and ducts.<SEP>Organ in the chest, site of breast cancer, often imaged and biopsied.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683079}, {"entity_name": "Bile Duct", "entity_type": "artifact", "description": "A tube that carries bile, made up of several layers including epithelium, lamina propria, muscle, perimuscular fibrous tissue, and serosa.<SEP>A duct made up of several layers including inner and outer membranes, with muscle and connective tissue in between.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Connective Tissue", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Connective Tissue is a fundamental biological material that serves to support, connect, and surround other tissues and structures within the body. It is found in a wide variety of anatomical locations, including the layers of the gallbladder, bile ducts, stomach wall, and bladder wall. In the context of the bladder, it is the specific layer of tissue that stage 1 cancer invades. Within the breast, connective tissue acts as a supporting framework that holds structures together, surrounds other components, and includes ligaments. Additionally, it is the tissue obtained from a skin punch biopsy, which is used to extract DNA for analysis. In pathology, connective tissue can also be fibrous tissue that some tumors form, and its presence in a tumor is an indicator of a high-risk or very high-risk cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682895}, {"entity_name": "Pelvic Floor", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A group of muscles supporting pelvic organs, involved in bowel, bladder, and sexual function.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-195", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Serosa", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "An outer membrane that covers the gallbladder and bile ducts, also called the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum.<SEP>A membrane also called the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, which is a thin layer covering a surface.<SEP>The outer layer of the colon wall, also called adventitia.<SEP>The outer membrane covering the stomach, allowing smooth movement against other organs.<SEP>The outermost layer of the stomach wall.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Dermis", "entity_type": "naturalobject", "description": "The middle layer of skin, containing connective tissue, blood vessels, oil and sweat glands, nerves, and hair follicles.<SEP>The middle layer of skin containing connective tissue, hair follicles, and sweat glands.<SEP>The layer of skin beneath the epidermis, partially removed during a superficial shave biopsy.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682760}, {"entity_name": "Membrane", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A very thin layer that covers a surface, such as the inner and outer layers of the bile duct.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Skin", "entity_type": "naturalobject", "description": "The body's largest organ, acting as a barrier, with three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.<SEP>The largest organ of the body, protecting against invaders, controlling body temperature, and allowing sensations. It has three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.<SEP>Tissue not affected by leukemia, used to test for predisposition syndromes.<SEP>A site where leukemia can spread outside the bloodstream.<SEP>The outer covering of the body, a site for HPV infection.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682760}, {"entity_name": "Bladder Wall", "entity_type": "location", "description": "The muscular wall of the bladder that can be invaded by cancer.<SEP>The wall of the bladder, composed of four main layers: inner lining (urothelial layer), connective tissue layer, muscle layer, and outer fatty layer.<SEP>The wall of the bladder, composed of layers including the lining, connective tissue, muscle, and fat.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Bladder", "tgt_id": "Bladder Cancer", "description": "Bladder cancer occurs in the bladder.<SEP>Bladder cancer originates in the bladder.<SEP>Bladder cancer is located in the bladder, which is examined during cystoscopy.", "keywords": "disease,located in,location,organ,origin", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Muscle Layer", "tgt_id": "Stomach Wall", "description": "The muscle layer is one of the five layers of the stomach wall.", "keywords": "anatomy,layers", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer", "description": "Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a phase of bladder cancer where the tumor has invaded the muscle layer.<SEP>Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a more advanced stage of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "phase,progression,stage", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Muscle Layer", "tgt_id": "Stomach", "description": "The stomach wall has a muscle layer.", "keywords": "has layer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer", "description": "Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a phase of bladder cancer where the cancer has not invaded the muscle layer.", "keywords": "phase,progression", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Muscle Layer", "tgt_id": "Subserosa", "description": "The muscle layer is surrounded by the subserosa.", "keywords": "adjacent to", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 2 Bladder Cancer", "description": "Stage 2 bladder cancer is muscle-invasive.", "keywords": "stage", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Connective Tissue", "description": "The breast contains connective tissue.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,part of,support", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Connective Tissue", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "The presence of connective tissue in a tumor is a sign of high-risk or very high-risk squamous cell skin cancer.", "keywords": "indicator,risk", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Serosa", "tgt_id": "Stomach Wall", "description": "The serosa is the outermost layer of the stomach wall.", "keywords": "anatomy,layers", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Breast Cancer", "description": "Breast cancer starts in the cells of the breast.<SEP>Breast cancer starts in the cells of the breast, often in the ducts or lobules.", "keywords": "affects,disease,originates in,starts in", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Dermis", "tgt_id": "Skin", "description": "The skin has a middle layer called the dermis.", "keywords": "has layer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683096}, {"src_id": "Gallbladder", "tgt_id": "Liver", "description": "The gallbladder is located near the liver.", "keywords": "near,related", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Epithelium", "tgt_id": "Ovaries", "description": "The epithelium is the surface layer of tissue surrounding the ovaries, where most ovarian cancers start.", "keywords": "anatomy,cancer origin", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Hepatobiliary System", "tgt_id": "Liver", "description": "The hepatobiliary system includes the liver.<SEP>The liver is part of the hepatobiliary system.", "keywords": "includes,part of", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Connective Tissue Layer", "tgt_id": "Stage 1", "description": "Stage 1 bladder cancer has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue layer.", "keywords": "cancer invasion,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bile", "tgt_id": "Liver", "description": "Liver cells make bile.<SEP>The liver makes bile.", "keywords": "makes,produces", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Gerota's Fascia", "tgt_id": "Kidneys", "description": "The kidney and adrenal gland are surrounded by a layer of fat, covered by Gerota's fascia.", "keywords": "anatomy,surrounded by", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Hepatic Ducts", "tgt_id": "Liver", "description": "Bile flows into small channels that drain into ducts, which form the hepatic ducts.", "keywords": "drains into", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Pathologist", "tgt_id": "Tumor Type", "description": "A pathologist analyzes tissue samples to determine the specific type and subtype of squamous cell skin cancer.", "keywords": "analysis,diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Lymph Vessels", "description": "The breast contains lymph vessels.<SEP>The breast contains lymph vessels that carry lymph fluid.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,lymphatic system", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Wall", "tgt_id": "Connective Tissue", "description": "Connective tissue is a layer of the bladder wall.", "keywords": "part of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder", "tgt_id": "PET Scan", "description": "The bladder normally appears bright on PET scans.", "keywords": "imaging,normal uptake", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Muscle (Muscularis)", "tgt_id": "Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue", "description": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer.", "keywords": "surrounds", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Glands", "tgt_id": "Head And Neck Cancer", "description": "Head and neck cancer may cause symptoms in the glands.", "keywords": "symptoms in", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Stomach Wall", "tgt_id": "Submucosa", "description": "The submucosa is the second layer of the stomach wall.", "keywords": "anatomy,layers", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Ducts", "description": "The breast contains ducts that connect lobules to the nipple.<SEP>The breast contains ducts.<SEP>The breast contains ducts that carry milk from the lobules to the nipple.<SEP>The breast contains milk ducts.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,milk transport,part of", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Epidermis", "tgt_id": "Skin", "description": "The skin has an outermost layer called the epidermis.", "keywords": "has layer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683096}, {"src_id": "Esophagus", "tgt_id": "Submucosa", "description": "The submucosa is a layer of connective tissue below the mucosa in the esophagus.", "keywords": "anatomy,layer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Lobules", "description": "The breast contains lobules, which are milk glands.<SEP>The breast contains lobules.<SEP>The breast contains lobules, which are glands that can make milk.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,milk production,part of", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Kidneys", "tgt_id": "Renal Capsule", "description": "The kidney is covered by a thin layer of tissue called the renal capsule.", "keywords": "anatomy,covered by", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Blood Vessels", "tgt_id": "Breast", "description": "The breast contains blood vessels.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,part of,vascular supply", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Hypodermis", "tgt_id": "Skin", "description": "The skin has a deepest layer called the hypodermis.", "keywords": "has layer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683096}, {"src_id": "Axillary Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Breast", "description": "Lymph from breast tissue drains into axillary lymph nodes.<SEP>Lymph fluid from breast tissue drains into axillary lymph nodes.<SEP>Lymph from breast tissue drains into lymph vessels and travels to axillary lymph nodes.", "keywords": "cancer spread,drains to,lymphatic drainage", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Breast", "tgt_id": "Fat", "description": "The breast contains fat.<SEP>The breast contains fat tissue.", "keywords": "anatomy,contains,part of", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Axillary Lymph Node", "tgt_id": "Breast", "description": "Lymph from breast tissue drains to axillary lymph nodes.", "keywords": "anatomy,drains to", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5111, "output_tokens": 16, "llm_calls": 1, "retrieval_time_ms": 756.0731000266969, "generation_time_ms": 1028.6362000042573, "total_time_ms": 1785.5379999382421, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["anatomy", "tissue layers"], "low_level": ["bile duct", "gallbladder", "muscle layer"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 77, "total_relations_found": 147, "entities_after_truncation": 18, "relations_after_truncation": 36, "merged_chunks_count": 36, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "What tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the bile duct and gallbladder?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer.", "evidence": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the bile duct.; Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer in the gallbladder.", "evidence_relations": "Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds the muscle layer", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-a4081e18", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the context, the performance measure evaluated to guide treatment decisions in primary CNS lymphoma is **Performance Status**.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Performance Status\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Performance Status is a clinical measure of a patient’s ability to perform daily activities, reflecting their overall well-being, general level of fitness, and capacity for self-care. It is commonly evaluated using standardized scales, such as the ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) scale, which assigns a score to quantify a person’s functional capability. This assessment is widely used in cancer care, including in the context of follicular lymphoma (FL), where it informs treatment planning and helps guide therapeutic decisions. Performance Status serves as a key indicator for determining the appropriate level of treatment intensity and is also used to assess a patient’s eligibility for clinical trials. By evaluating how well a patient can manage ordinary day-to-day tasks, this metric provides clinicians with essential information to tailor care and predict a patient’s ability to tolerate specific interventions.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in lymphocytes within the central nervous system (CNS). It most commonly affects the brain, often developing deep within brain tissue, but can also occur in the spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and the eyes. The disease can spread within the nervous system but rarely metastasizes to other areas of the body. Diagnosis typically requires a biopsy and imaging tests to confirm the presence of the malignancy.\\n\\nAs a specific type of lymphoma confined to the CNS, it is the subject of various therapeutic approaches discussed in medical literature. Treatment options for this condition are actively explored, with multiple therapies being considered or applied to manage the disease. The cancer's aggressive nature and its location within the CNS make it a challenging condition to treat, necessitating specialized medical intervention.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683084}, {\"entity_name\": \"Treatment Team\", \"entity_type\": \"organization\", \"description\": \"Treatment Team is a group of healthcare professionals involved in a patient's cancer care, responsible for providing comprehensive care and guidance. This team typically includes doctors, nurses, and specialists, such as urologists, who collaborate to manage the patient's condition. Their primary duties involve working with the patient to determine the most appropriate therapies for their specific cancer type, following established best practices. The team also plays a key role in recommending and conducting biomarker testing, using biopsy results to inform treatment planning, and deciding on suitable therapeutic approaches. Additionally, they are tasked with managing the patient's cancer treatment and associated side effects, while providing essential information on these topics to ensure the patient is well-informed throughout their care journey.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682894}, {\"entity_name\": \"Clinical Trial\", \"entity_type\": \"event\", \"description\": \"Clinical Trial is a form of medical research study conducted with human participants to evaluate new treatments, procedures, or interventions—including medical, surgical, and behavioral approaches—with the primary goal of determining their safety, effectiveness, and potential benefits. These studies are central to advancing cancer care, as they test investigational drugs, devices, or treatment strategies, such as chemotherapy, targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and other systemic therapies, often comparing them against standard treatments or placebos. Clinical trials are typically offered as an optional or recommended pathway for patients across various cancer types, including cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), primary CNS lymphoma, metastatic thyroid cancer, glioma, bladder cancer, anal cancer, and metastatic anal cancer, among others. Participation may allow patients access to novel therapies while contributing to medical knowledge, and in some contexts, such as metastatic thyroid cancer or anal cancer, enrollment is strongly encouraged when trials are available.\\n\\nThe design and conduct of clinical trials are governed by structured protocols that include defined phases—commonly phases 2, 3, and 4—which assess treatment efficacy and safety at different stages of investigation. Trials are characterized by specific eligibility criteria, informed consent processes, and considerations regarding potential risks, benefits, and costs, though enrollment is often offered at no fee. These studies may involve placebos or investigational drugs and are conducted after laboratory development has shown promise. If a treatment proves safe and effective, it may receive approval from regulatory bodies such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Clinical trials serve dual purposes: they enable patients to receive cutting-edge care and allow researchers to gather critical data on disease management, ultimately improving future treatment standards.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682888}, {\"entity_name\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, specifically the brain and spinal cord.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Treatment team\", \"entity_type\": \"organization\", \"description\": \"The group of healthcare professionals involved in a patient's care.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Phases of Treatment\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The stages of cancer treatment, including induction and consolidation, as mentioned in the text.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Targeted therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A treatment approach that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth, such as bevacizumab or mirvetuximab.<SEP>A cancer treatment that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683056}, {\"entity_name\": \"Targeted Therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Targeted Therapy is a form of systemic cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to identify and attack specific molecules, genes, proteins, or receptors involved in the growth, spread, and survival of cancer cells. Unlike broad-spectrum treatments like chemotherapy, this approach focuses on particular genetic changes, mutations (e.g., PIK3CA), or biomarkers (e.g., PARP inhibitors) present in tumors, making it effective for cancers with certain molecular features. By targeting these specific parts of cancer cells, the therapy aims to slow or stop tumor progression, and it may potentially cause fewer side effects than chemotherapy, though it can also be combined with chemotherapy in certain contexts, such as for primary CNS lymphoma.\\n\\nThis therapy is applicable across various cancer types, including advanced-stage prostate cancer, recurrent and/or metastatic cervical cancer, and thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy, have recurred, or have metastasized and continue to grow. For thyroid cancer, the targeted therapies currently used are kinase inhibitors, typically administered as swallowed capsules. However, the treatment is not without risks; side effects can include body aches, rash, high blood pressure, and abnormal bleeding, and some agents may adversely affect the heart, skin, and digestive system. Additionally, targeted therapy can impact fertility, and its use is often guided by biomarker testing to determine patient eligibility.\\n\\nAs a systemic therapy, it is generally reserved for cases where specific genetic targets are identified, and it may be employed when other treatments have failed or are unsuitable. The overarching goal remains to attack specific types of cancer cells while minimizing harm to normal cells, thereby offering a more precise treatment option for individuals with particular gene mutations or molecular profiles.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682881}, {\"entity_name\": \"Radiation therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A cancer treatment that uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Clinical trial\", \"entity_type\": \"event\", \"description\": \"A research study testing new treatments; encouraged for patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer.<SEP>A research study that tests new treatments in people.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683054}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Performance Status\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Performance status evaluation helps determine the level of treatment a patient with primary CNS lymphoma can handle.\", \"keywords\": \"assessment,treatment planning\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Treatment Team\", \"description\": \"The treatment team manages the patient's care and decides on treatments for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"care,management\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Clinical Trial\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Treatment in a clinical trial is a recommended option for primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>NCCN experts recommend that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial.<SEP>Clinical trials are a recommended treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Patients with primary CNS lymphoma are encouraged to consider enrolling in clinical trials.\", \"keywords\": \"recommended option,research,treated in,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Treatment team\", \"description\": \"The treatment team discusses results and treatment options with the patient.\", \"keywords\": \"care,management\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Phases of Treatment\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Treatment for primary CNS lymphoma involves phases, including induction and consolidation.\", \"keywords\": \"has\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Targeted therapy\", \"description\": \"Targeted therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Targeted Therapy\", \"description\": \"Targeted therapy is another treatment for primary CNS lymphoma that can be combined with chemotherapy.<SEP>Targeted therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Clinical trial\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Clinical trials are recommended for everyone with primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Consolidation Phase\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with a consolidation phase as the second step.\", \"keywords\": \"phase,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Immunotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Immunotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Remission\", \"description\": \"Treatment may put primary CNS lymphoma into remission.\", \"keywords\": \"can achieve\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Biopsy is the main test to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"NCCN\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"NCCN experts recommend that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial.\", \"keywords\": \"recommendation,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Immunotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Immunotherapy is used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, with rituximab as the main drug.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Supportive care\", \"description\": \"Supportive care is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Head And Neck Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Performance Status\", \"description\": \"Performance status is a factor in choosing a treatment plan for head and neck cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"factor,treatment planning\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Induction Phase\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with an induction phase as the first step.\", \"keywords\": \"phase,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Corticosteroids\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Corticosteroids are a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment option\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, often given in combination or with stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method for primary CNS lymphoma, often using drugs like methotrexate.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill lymphoma cells, and high-dose chemotherapy is part of stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treated with,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with radiation therapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is an option for treating primary CNS lymphoma, especially for those who cannot have chemotherapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, including in the eyes.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,radiation,treated with,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Imaging tests\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Imaging tests show areas that might have cancer and the extent of the disease.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis,extent\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Treatment for the Eyes\", \"description\": \"Treatment for primary CNS lymphoma may include treatment for the eyes.\", \"keywords\": \"may involve\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Follicular Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Performance Status\", \"description\": \"Performance status is assessed as part of general health tests for follicular lymphoma patients.\", \"keywords\": \"consideration in treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Corticosteroids\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Corticosteroids can shrink lymphomas and decrease brain swelling in primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Corticosteroids are one of the treatment options for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"symptom relief,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Neurological Exam\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"A neurological exam is an important test to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>A neurological exam is part of the diagnostic process for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"assessment,diagnosed by,diagnosis\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Surgery\", \"description\": \"Surgery is not normally an option for primary CNS lymphoma because it develops deep within brain tissue.\", \"keywords\": \"not recommended,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Performance Status (PS)\", \"tgt_id\": \"Treatment Plan\", \"description\": \"Performance status is one factor taken into consideration when choosing a treatment plan.\", \"keywords\": \"consideration,treatment decision\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stem Cell Transplant\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with stem cell transplant.\", \"keywords\": \"treated with\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Blood Tests\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Blood tests are used in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Blood tests help establish the extent of cancer and provide information for treatment planning.\", \"keywords\": \"assesses extent,diagnosed by\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Induction Phase\", \"tgt_id\": \"Methotrexate\", \"description\": \"High-dose methotrexate is a recommended induction treatment for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"chemotherapy,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Performance Status", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Performance Status is a clinical measure of a patient’s ability to perform daily activities, reflecting their overall well-being, general level of fitness, and capacity for self-care. It is commonly evaluated using standardized scales, such as the ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) scale, which assigns a score to quantify a person’s functional capability. This assessment is widely used in cancer care, including in the context of follicular lymphoma (FL), where it informs treatment planning and helps guide therapeutic decisions. Performance Status serves as a key indicator for determining the appropriate level of treatment intensity and is also used to assess a patient’s eligibility for clinical trials. By evaluating how well a patient can manage ordinary day-to-day tasks, this metric provides clinicians with essential information to tailor care and predict a patient’s ability to tolerate specific interventions.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in lymphocytes within the central nervous system (CNS). It most commonly affects the brain, often developing deep within brain tissue, but can also occur in the spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and the eyes. The disease can spread within the nervous system but rarely metastasizes to other areas of the body. Diagnosis typically requires a biopsy and imaging tests to confirm the presence of the malignancy.\n\nAs a specific type of lymphoma confined to the CNS, it is the subject of various therapeutic approaches discussed in medical literature. Treatment options for this condition are actively explored, with multiple therapies being considered or applied to manage the disease. The cancer's aggressive nature and its location within the CNS make it a challenging condition to treat, necessitating specialized medical intervention.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683084}, {"entity_name": "Treatment Team", "entity_type": "organization", "description": "Treatment Team is a group of healthcare professionals involved in a patient's cancer care, responsible for providing comprehensive care and guidance. This team typically includes doctors, nurses, and specialists, such as urologists, who collaborate to manage the patient's condition. Their primary duties involve working with the patient to determine the most appropriate therapies for their specific cancer type, following established best practices. The team also plays a key role in recommending and conducting biomarker testing, using biopsy results to inform treatment planning, and deciding on suitable therapeutic approaches. Additionally, they are tasked with managing the patient's cancer treatment and associated side effects, while providing essential information on these topics to ensure the patient is well-informed throughout their care journey.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682894}, {"entity_name": "Clinical Trial", "entity_type": "event", "description": "Clinical Trial is a form of medical research study conducted with human participants to evaluate new treatments, procedures, or interventions—including medical, surgical, and behavioral approaches—with the primary goal of determining their safety, effectiveness, and potential benefits. These studies are central to advancing cancer care, as they test investigational drugs, devices, or treatment strategies, such as chemotherapy, targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and other systemic therapies, often comparing them against standard treatments or placebos. Clinical trials are typically offered as an optional or recommended pathway for patients across various cancer types, including cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), primary CNS lymphoma, metastatic thyroid cancer, glioma, bladder cancer, anal cancer, and metastatic anal cancer, among others. Participation may allow patients access to novel therapies while contributing to medical knowledge, and in some contexts, such as metastatic thyroid cancer or anal cancer, enrollment is strongly encouraged when trials are available.\n\nThe design and conduct of clinical trials are governed by structured protocols that include defined phases—commonly phases 2, 3, and 4—which assess treatment efficacy and safety at different stages of investigation. Trials are characterized by specific eligibility criteria, informed consent processes, and considerations regarding potential risks, benefits, and costs, though enrollment is often offered at no fee. These studies may involve placebos or investigational drugs and are conducted after laboratory development has shown promise. If a treatment proves safe and effective, it may receive approval from regulatory bodies such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Clinical trials serve dual purposes: they enable patients to receive cutting-edge care and allow researchers to gather critical data on disease management, ultimately improving future treatment standards.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682888}, {"entity_name": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, specifically the brain and spinal cord.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Treatment team", "entity_type": "organization", "description": "The group of healthcare professionals involved in a patient's care.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Phases of Treatment", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The stages of cancer treatment, including induction and consolidation, as mentioned in the text.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Targeted therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A treatment approach that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth, such as bevacizumab or mirvetuximab.<SEP>A cancer treatment that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683056}, {"entity_name": "Targeted Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Targeted Therapy is a form of systemic cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to identify and attack specific molecules, genes, proteins, or receptors involved in the growth, spread, and survival of cancer cells. Unlike broad-spectrum treatments like chemotherapy, this approach focuses on particular genetic changes, mutations (e.g., PIK3CA), or biomarkers (e.g., PARP inhibitors) present in tumors, making it effective for cancers with certain molecular features. By targeting these specific parts of cancer cells, the therapy aims to slow or stop tumor progression, and it may potentially cause fewer side effects than chemotherapy, though it can also be combined with chemotherapy in certain contexts, such as for primary CNS lymphoma.\n\nThis therapy is applicable across various cancer types, including advanced-stage prostate cancer, recurrent and/or metastatic cervical cancer, and thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy, have recurred, or have metastasized and continue to grow. For thyroid cancer, the targeted therapies currently used are kinase inhibitors, typically administered as swallowed capsules. However, the treatment is not without risks; side effects can include body aches, rash, high blood pressure, and abnormal bleeding, and some agents may adversely affect the heart, skin, and digestive system. Additionally, targeted therapy can impact fertility, and its use is often guided by biomarker testing to determine patient eligibility.\n\nAs a systemic therapy, it is generally reserved for cases where specific genetic targets are identified, and it may be employed when other treatments have failed or are unsuitable. The overarching goal remains to attack specific types of cancer cells while minimizing harm to normal cells, thereby offering a more precise treatment option for individuals with particular gene mutations or molecular profiles.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682881}, {"entity_name": "Radiation therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A cancer treatment that uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Clinical trial", "entity_type": "event", "description": "A research study testing new treatments; encouraged for patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer.<SEP>A research study that tests new treatments in people.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683054}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Performance Status", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Performance status evaluation helps determine the level of treatment a patient with primary CNS lymphoma can handle.", "keywords": "assessment,treatment planning", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Treatment Team", "description": "The treatment team manages the patient's care and decides on treatments for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "care,management", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Clinical Trial", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Treatment in a clinical trial is a recommended option for primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>NCCN experts recommend that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial.<SEP>Clinical trials are a recommended treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Patients with primary CNS lymphoma are encouraged to consider enrolling in clinical trials.", "keywords": "recommended option,research,treated in,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Treatment team", "description": "The treatment team discusses results and treatment options with the patient.", "keywords": "care,management", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Phases of Treatment", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Treatment for primary CNS lymphoma involves phases, including induction and consolidation.", "keywords": "has", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Targeted therapy", "description": "Targeted therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Targeted Therapy", "description": "Targeted therapy is another treatment for primary CNS lymphoma that can be combined with chemotherapy.<SEP>Targeted therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Radiation therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Clinical trial", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Clinical trials are recommended for everyone with primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Consolidation Phase", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with a consolidation phase as the second step.", "keywords": "phase,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Immunotherapy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Immunotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Remission", "description": "Treatment may put primary CNS lymphoma into remission.", "keywords": "can achieve", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Biopsy is the main test to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "NCCN", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "NCCN experts recommend that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial.", "keywords": "recommendation,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Immunotherapy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Immunotherapy is used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, with rituximab as the main drug.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Supportive care", "description": "Supportive care is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Head And Neck Cancer", "tgt_id": "Performance Status", "description": "Performance status is a factor in choosing a treatment plan for head and neck cancer.", "keywords": "factor,treatment planning", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Induction Phase", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with an induction phase as the first step.", "keywords": "phase,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Corticosteroids", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Corticosteroids are a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "treatment option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, often given in combination or with stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method for primary CNS lymphoma, often using drugs like methotrexate.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill lymphoma cells, and high-dose chemotherapy is part of stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "cancer,treated with,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with radiation therapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is an option for treating primary CNS lymphoma, especially for those who cannot have chemotherapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, including in the eyes.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "cancer,radiation,treated with,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Imaging tests", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "description": "Imaging tests show areas that might have cancer and the extent of the disease.", "keywords": "diagnosis,extent", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Treatment for the Eyes", "description": "Treatment for primary CNS lymphoma may include treatment for the eyes.", "keywords": "may involve", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Follicular Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Performance Status", "description": "Performance status is assessed as part of general health tests for follicular lymphoma patients.", "keywords": "consideration in treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Corticosteroids", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Corticosteroids can shrink lymphomas and decrease brain swelling in primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Corticosteroids are one of the treatment options for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "symptom relief,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Neurological Exam", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "A neurological exam is an important test to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>A neurological exam is part of the diagnostic process for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "assessment,diagnosed by,diagnosis", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is not normally an option for primary CNS lymphoma because it develops deep within brain tissue.", "keywords": "not recommended,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Performance Status (PS)", "tgt_id": "Treatment Plan", "description": "Performance status is one factor taken into consideration when choosing a treatment plan.", "keywords": "consideration,treatment decision", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Stem Cell Transplant", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with stem cell transplant.", "keywords": "treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Blood Tests", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Blood tests are used in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>Blood tests help establish the extent of cancer and provide information for treatment planning.", "keywords": "assesses extent,diagnosed by", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Induction Phase", "tgt_id": "Methotrexate", "description": "High-dose methotrexate is a recommended induction treatment for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "chemotherapy,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5822, "output_tokens": 52, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1827.5370000628754, "generation_time_ms": 778.4634999698028, "total_time_ms": 2606.6707000136375, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["performance measure", "treatment decisions", "primary CNS lymphoma"], "low_level": []}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 38, "total_relations_found": 40, "entities_after_truncation": 11, "relations_after_truncation": 32, "merged_chunks_count": 29, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "Which performance measure is evaluated to guide treatment decisions in primary CNS lymphoma?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Performance status is evaluated to guide treatment decisions and eligibility for clinical trials.", "evidence": "Performance status is evaluated to guide treatment decisions.; Performance status is evaluated to determine eligibility for clinical trials.", "evidence_relations": "Performance status is evaluated to guide treatment decisions and eligibility for clinical trials", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-ff590b8b", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the context, treatments for advanced-stage prostate cancer include chemotherapy, hormone therapy, radiation therapy, and radiopharmaceuticals.", "contexts": ["and stays within the prostate. This is also called localized prostate cancer. Advanced-stage prostate cancer has grown outside the prostate and may have spread to other areas in the body such as lymph nodes (bottom left), bones, or organs like the liver or lungs (bottom right). This is also called metastatic prostate cancer. Can prostate cancer be cured? Early-stage prostate cancer is highly treatable and often curable. The earlier that prostate cancer is diagnosed and treated, the more likely that a patient will live without cancer. Most people with early-stage disease live without cancer for many years, usually the rest of their lives. Scientists have learned a great deal about prostate cancer in recent years. As a result, today’s detection methods and treatments work better than those in the past. Also, many patients with prostate cancer have more treatment choices now than they had before. Treatments for early-stage prostate cancer include surgery, radiation therapy, and hormone therapy, among others. However, some people with prostate cancer don’t need to be treated right away. Many patients with early-stage prostate cancer can have their cancer managed with active surveillance. During active surveillance, you’ll have regular tests to keep an eye on your cancer. But you won’t have treatment unless the cancer grows or changes in a way that requires treatment. The goal of active surveillance is to avoid the potential side effects of treatment, with the option for treatment in the future if you need it. Some patients on active surveillance may never have to be treated. Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread to other areas of the pelvis (regional prostate cancer) or the body (metastatic prostate cancer), and it's usually a life-long disease. But treatment can slow down its growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong your life. Treatments for advanced-stage prostate cancer include hormone therapy, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, surgery, and other therapies. Early detection and treatment can greatly reduce the chances of getting advanced-stage prostate cancer. Many people with advancedstage prostate cancer continue to live their lives with the cancer and, in the end, may die from something else. Something to remember: When found early, prostate cancer has one of the highest survival rates of any cancer. Key points Prostate cancer develops when cells in the prostate gland grow out of control. Age is the biggest risk factor for prostate cancer. As you age, your chances of developing prostate cancer increase. Everyone with a prostate is at risk for prostate cancer. Not everyone with prostate cancer has symptoms. For most people, prostate cancer usually grows slowly and stays within the prostate. Early-stage prostate cancer hasn’t spread outside the prostate gland. Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread outside the prostate to other areas in the body. This spread is called metastasis. Not everyone diagnosed with prostate cancer needs treatment. 66 Be your own advocate. Talk to someone who has gone through the same thing as you. Ask a lot of questions, even the ones you are afraid to ask. You have to protect yourself and ensure you make the best decisions for you, and get the best care for your particular situation.” 2 Tests for prostate cancer About testing Common prostate tests General health tests Diagnostic tests What’s next? Key points If you haven’t yet been diagnosed, testing is necessary to find out if you have prostate cancer and whether it’s early-stage or advanced-stage cancer. Testing also helps your providers plan the right treatment. About testing Health care providers use a variety of tests to find prostate cancer and to determine how advanced the cancer is. Tests are used to plan treatment, check how well treatment is working, and monitor your health after treatment ends. This chapter will help you know what tests you may have and what to expect during testing. When you go for testing, bring someone with you to listen, ask questions, and write down the answers. Testing begins with prostate cancer screening tests, followed by tests of your general health, and then diagnostic tests, if needed. Not every person with prostate cancer will receive every test listed here. When you go for testing, bring someone with you to listen, ask questions, and write down the answers. Common prostate tests Common prostate cancer tests are used to detect the possibility of prostate cancer in someone who’s undiagnosed. They’re also used to check and monitor prostate cancer in patients who have been diagnosed. Two common tests that look for prostate cancer are the prostate-specific antigen (PSA) test and digital rectal exam. PSA test This is a blood test that measures the amount of PSA in your bloodstream. PSA is a protein made inside the prostate gland. Its job is to help semen transport sperm. All prostate cells, both normal cells and cancer cells, make PSA. If there’s something wrong with the prostate— like prostate cancer—the prostate may make more PSA. While most PSA goes into semen, a little bit ends up in the bloodstream, too. An unusually high amount of PSA in the blood may be a sign of prostate cancer. Likewise, an increase in PSA after treatment may indicate that the treatment is losing effectiveness. However, age and other factors—such as an enlarged prostate or a urinary tract infection— can also cause high levels of PSA. This means that a PSA test by itself can’t provide a diagnosis of prostate cancer. That’s why a PSA test is often paired with imaging or a digital rectal exam, or both, to decide whether you need a biopsy. PSA level (also called total PSA) is measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. PSA testing Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is a protein made inside the prostate gland. If there’s something wrong with the prostate, the prostate may make more PSA. An unusually high amount of PSA in the blood may be a sign of prostate cancer. But a high PSA level doesn’t automatically mean you have prostate cancer. Rather, it’s a warning sign that you may need further testing. Digital rectal exam Don’t be fooled by the name—no high-tech electronics are used in"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Metastatic Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Metastatic Cancer is a term used to describe cancer that has spread from its original (primary) site to other, distant parts of the body. This process of spread can involve various organs and tissues, with common distant sites including the lungs, liver, bones, and brain, as well as distant lymph nodes. The condition is often characterized as very advanced disease, and when cancer is identified as stage 4 at the time of diagnosis, it is considered metastatic from the outset. In specific contexts, such as esophageal cancer, the metastatic stage specifically denotes that the disease has spread to distant bodily locations. Regardless of the primary tumor's origin, the defining feature of metastatic cancer is its presence in areas far removed from the initial site of development.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682934}, {"entity_name": "Systemic Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Systemic Therapy is a broad category of cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to reach and affect cancer cells throughout the body, rather than targeting a single localized area. These therapies are typically administered intravenously or orally, allowing the medicine to travel through the bloodstream to reach cells in any part of the body. This approach is used to treat various cancers, including basal cell skin cancer, acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), Ewing sarcoma, metastatic cervical cancer, and bladder cancer, among others. Systemic therapy can be employed as a primary treatment, as an initial therapy, or before or after surgery (neoadjuvant or adjuvant settings), and may also be used as maintenance therapy to prevent recurrence.\n\nThe main types of systemic therapy include chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy. Chemotherapy uses drugs to kill rapidly dividing cancer cells, while targeted therapy focuses on specific molecules involved in cancer growth, such as HER2-targeted agents or inhibitors. Immunotherapy harnesses the body’s immune system to fight cancer. Other forms include endocrine (hormonal) therapy, which is often used in hormone-sensitive cancers, and platinum-based chemotherapy regimens. These treatments can be used alone or in combination, depending on the cancer type and stage.\n\nBecause systemic therapy affects cells throughout the body, it can impact both cancer cells and normal cells, leading to a range of potential side effects. Common short-term side effects include tiredness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, hair loss, mouth sores, loss of appetite, and low blood cell counts. Some side effects may be delayed and occur years after treatment. Additionally, certain systemic therapies, particularly some targeted treatments, can affect fertility. The specific side effects and their severity vary based on the drugs used, the dosage, and the individual patient.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-109<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-110<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682772}, {"entity_name": "Prostate Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Prostate Cancer is a disease in which cells in the prostate gland grow out of control, and it is one of the most common types of cancer. The condition can be early-stage (localized) or advanced-stage (metastatic), with the latter occurring when cancer spreads outside the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes and bones, through a process called metastasis. Prostate cancer is often slow-growing and asymptomatic in its early stages, contributing to high survival rates when detected early. Risk factors for developing the disease include age, family history, genetic factors, race, and lifestyle. Detection and risk assessment involve various tests, including PSA (prostate-specific antigen) levels, biopsy, and grading, and the disease often requires staging and treatment. The condition is also a focus of risk assessment and genetic testing discussions, as it is characterized by the presence of prostate cancer cells and abnormal cell growth within the gland.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683075}, {"entity_name": "Treatment", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Medical care given to a patient for cancer, which can affect fertility.<SEP>Medical care given to a patient for an illness or condition.<SEP>Medical care aimed at stopping the activity of the BCR::ABL1 protein in CML and treating ALL.<SEP>Medical interventions such as surgery or radiation therapy for prostate cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683063}, {"entity_name": "First-Line Therapy", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The initial treatment approach for metastatic anal cancer, typically platinum-based chemotherapy.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Anal cancer that has spread to other parts of the body (stage IV), treated with systemic therapy.<SEP>Anal cancer that has spread to distant areas such as the liver or lungs.<SEP>Anal cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, most often to the liver or lungs.<SEP>Anal cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, often treated with radiation and chemotherapy.<SEP>Anal cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, treated with chemotherapy and possibly immunotherapy.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "RAI Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Radioactive iodine therapy, a treatment for thyroid cancer, mentioned as a standard treatment that targeted therapy is used when RAI therapy cannot be performed.<SEP>Radioactive iodine therapy used to destroy remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells after surgery.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Metastatic Cancer", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Systemic therapy is used to treat metastatic colon cancer.", "keywords": "advanced,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Treatment", "description": "Treatment for prostate cancer depends on the risk group and may include surgery or radiation therapy.", "keywords": "management,therapy", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "First-Line Therapy", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "description": "Metastatic anal cancer is treated first with platinum-based chemotherapy, which is called first-line therapy.", "keywords": "treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cancer", "tgt_id": "RAI Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is recommended for metastatic papillary, follicular, or oncocytic thyroid cancers that take up iodine.", "keywords": "metastatic,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "description": "Metastatic anal cancer is treated with chemotherapy.", "keywords": "treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Advanced prostate cancer can be treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "non-hormone,treated by,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Hormone Therapy", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer can be treated with hormone therapy.<SEP>Hormone therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Hormone therapy is a systemic treatment for prostate cancer that adds, blocks, or removes hormones.<SEP>Hormone therapy has been the main treatment for advanced prostate cancer for decades.", "keywords": "mainstay,systemic,treated by,treatment", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Metastatic anal cancer is treated with systemic therapy, such as chemotherapy or immunotherapy.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Platinum-Based Chemotherapy", "description": "Metastatic cervical cancer is usually treated with platinum-based chemotherapy.", "keywords": "systemic therapy,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Carboplatin And Paclitaxel", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "description": "Carboplatin and paclitaxel is a first-line chemotherapy option for metastatic anal cancer.", "keywords": "first-line,treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiopharmaceuticals", "description": "Radiopharmaceuticals are a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Prostate cancer can be treated with radiation therapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment option for prostate cancer, often used for higher-risk cases.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "management,non-hormone,treated by,treatment", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "PSMA-PET", "tgt_id": "Targeted Therapy", "description": "PSMA-PET is used to determine whether targeted therapy can treat certain metastatic prostate cancer cases.", "keywords": "treatment planning", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Metastatic cervical cancer is usually treated with systemic therapy.", "keywords": "treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cancer", "tgt_id": "Targeted Therapy", "description": "Targeted therapy may be an option for metastatic cancer that does not take up iodine.", "keywords": "metastatic,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Prostate cancer can be treated with surgery.<SEP>Surgery is a treatment option for prostate cancer, often used for higher-risk cases.<SEP>Surgery is a treatment option for advanced-stage prostate cancer.", "keywords": "management,treated by,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "5-FU And Cisplatin", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "description": "5-FU and cisplatin is a first-line chemotherapy option for metastatic anal cancer.", "keywords": "first-line,treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Targeted Therapy", "description": "Targeted therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Targeted therapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "non-hormone,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Topotecan", "description": "Topotecan is used in combination regimens for metastatic cervical cancer.", "keywords": "combination,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is used to treat metastatic anal cancer.", "keywords": "treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Keytruda", "tgt_id": "Padcev", "description": "The preferred therapy for metastatic bladder cancer is a combination of Padcev and Keytruda.", "keywords": "combination,preferred therapy", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Resection", "description": "Metastatic growths may be removed via resection.", "keywords": "may be treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cisplatin", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "description": "Cisplatin is used for metastatic cervical cancer, typically given once every 3 weeks.", "keywords": "treatment for", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Ablative Therapies", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "description": "Ablative therapies can destroy metastatic lesions.", "keywords": "may be treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy", "tgt_id": "Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)", "description": "Metastatic or unresectable TMB-H cancers are often treated with immune checkpoint inhibitors.", "keywords": "indication,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Tisotumab Vedotin-Tftv", "description": "Tisotumab vedotin-tftv is a preferred option for metastatic cervical cancers without certain biomarkers.", "keywords": "option,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Immunotherapy", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Immunotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "non-hormone,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy", "description": "SBRT is used to treat metastatic cervical cancer.", "keywords": "treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Cervical Cancer", "description": "EBRT may be used to treat metastatic lesions.", "keywords": "may be treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer and as part of chemoradiation.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation therapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treats", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5798, "output_tokens": 55, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1486.0743000172079, "generation_time_ms": 1262.4545999569818, "total_time_ms": 2748.918000026606, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["treatments", "metastatic prostate cancer"], "low_level": []}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 39, "total_relations_found": 40, "entities_after_truncation": 7, "relations_after_truncation": 30, "merged_chunks_count": 28, "final_chunks_count": 1}}, "question": "Which treatments are available for metastatic prostate cancer?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Metastatic prostate cancer treatments include ADT, LHRH agonist/antagonist, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapy, radiation therapy, and radiopharmaceuticals.", "evidence": "ADT is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; LHRH agonist/antagonist is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; Chemotherapy is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; Immunotherapy is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; Targeted therapy is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; Radiation therapy is a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.; Radiopharmaceuticals are a treatment for metastatic prostate cancer.", "evidence_relations": "Metastatic prostate cancer: Treatment: Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT), LHRH agonist, LHRH antagonist, Leuprolide, Goserelin, Triptorelin, Degarelix, Relugolix, Bicalutamide, Flutamide, Nilutamide, Orchiectomy, Chemotherapy, Immunotherapy, Targeted therapy, Radiation therapy, Radiopharmaceuticals", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-4cfb5a3a", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the provided context, genetic tests can identify hereditary conditions that increase adrenal tumor risk. However, the context does not specify which particular genetic tests are performed for this purpose. Therefore, I don't know.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Genetic Test\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A test used to identify genetic mutations associated with hereditary conditions.<SEP>A test to identify abnormal changes in genes, used to assess cancer risk and guide treatment.<SEP>A test to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes, including germline testing.<SEP>A test used to find harmful changes in genes.<SEP>A test to identify mutations in genes.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682801}, {\"entity_name\": \"Hereditary Condition\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A genetic mutation passed from parent to child that can increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682801}, {\"entity_name\": \"Breast Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Breast Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in breast tissue, most commonly originating in the epithelial cells of the terminal duct lobular units, specifically within the ducts or lobules. The vast majority of breast cancers are carcinomas. This malignant disease can spread to surrounding tissue and other parts of the body, such as the lungs; when it metastasizes, it is still referred to as breast cancer.\\n\\nThe condition is frequently associated with germline mutations, particularly in the BRCA genes, and can be hereditary in approximately 1 out of 10 cases. A personal or family history of breast cancer may also be linked to an increased risk of developing other cancers, such as prostate cancer and thyroid cancer. Clinical discussions of breast cancer often focus on staging, prognostic factors, and the use of biomarker testing and tumor markers for diagnosis and management.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682946}, {\"entity_name\": \"Hereditary Genetic Testing\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Testing for inherited gene mutations using blood or saliva; also referred to as genetic risk testing.<SEP>Testing to identify inherited genetic mutations that increase the risk of cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683079}, {\"entity_name\": \"Adrenal Tumor\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A tumor that can be found in one or both adrenal glands; most are benign, but some can be cancerous.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682801}, {\"entity_name\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A rare cancer that starts in one or both adrenal glands, affecting approximately 1 to 2 people per million.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682796}, {\"entity_name\": \"Familial Adenomatous Polyposis\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A hereditary condition that may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor.<SEP>An inherited syndrome causing numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, often leading to colon cancer.<SEP>An inherited syndrome related to colon cancer.<SEP>An inherited syndrome related to thyroid cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682796}, {\"entity_name\": \"Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"An ethnic background associated with higher risk of certain genetic mutations, including BRCA1 and BRCA2.<SEP>An ethnic background associated with higher risk of certain genetic mutations.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683079}, {\"entity_name\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Prostate Cancer is a disease in which cells in the prostate gland grow out of control, and it is one of the most common types of cancer. The condition can be early-stage (localized) or advanced-stage (metastatic), with the latter occurring when cancer spreads outside the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes and bones, through a process called metastasis. Prostate cancer is often slow-growing and asymptomatic in its early stages, contributing to high survival rates when detected early. Risk factors for developing the disease include age, family history, genetic factors, race, and lifestyle. Detection and risk assessment involve various tests, including PSA (prostate-specific antigen) levels, biopsy, and grading, and the disease often requires staging and treatment. The condition is also a focus of risk assessment and genetic testing discussions, as it is characterized by the presence of prostate cancer cells and abnormal cell growth within the gland.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683075}, {\"entity_name\": \"Family History\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Family History is a comprehensive record of the health information of an individual's blood relatives, including parents, siblings, children, grandparents, aunts, uncles, nieces, nephews, and half-siblings. This information encompasses the presence of various diseases and health conditions, such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes, along with the age at which relatives were diagnosed. It serves as a critical tool in clinical practice, used to assess an individual's risk for inherited conditions and to guide healthcare decisions, treatment plans, and preventive care.\\n\\nThe utility of Family History is particularly pronounced in oncology. It is a key risk factor for several types of cancer, including basal cell skin cancer, prostate cancer, thyroid cancer, and bladder cancer. For instance, having a close relative (like a parent or sibling) with prostate or bladder cancer increases an individual's chance of developing the disease. Furthermore, a detailed history of cancer occurrences in family members, including the specific type of cancer and age of onset, is essential for identifying potential hereditary cancer syndromes. This information can prompt healthcare providers to recommend genetic testing and risk assessment to determine if an individual carries a higher genetic predisposition for certain malignancies, such as chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682799}, {\"entity_name\": \"Genetic Testing\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Genetic Testing is a medical diagnostic process that analyzes DNA to identify inherited (germline) mutations, typically performed on normal tissue samples such as blood, saliva, cheek swabs, or urine. This form of testing is distinct from tumor mutation testing, as it focuses on determining whether an individual carries hereditary genetic changes that increase their risk for developing cancer. It is commonly used to detect mutations in BRCA genes and other cancer-related genes, and is recommended for all individuals diagnosed with ovarian cancer to guide clinical management.\\n\\nThe scope of genetic testing extends beyond hereditary risk assessment. In oncology, it is also utilized to analyze genetic changes and biomarkers within cancer cells—such as leukemia cells—to inform treatment decisions and prognosis. Additionally, genetic testing serves as a method to identify specific mutations linked to prostate cancer and other malignancies, helping to tailor therapeutic approaches. The test can be performed on various sample types, including blood, saliva, urine, or tumor tissue, depending on the clinical question.\\n\\nGenetic testing is also valuable for identifying mutations that cause inherited cancer syndromes, and results are often relevant for family members, as knowing whether a relative has undergone testing can influence interpretation of one’s own results. Overall, genetic testing plays a critical role in cancer risk assessment, early detection, and personalized treatment planning across multiple cancer types.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683022}, {\"entity_name\": \"Genetic Tests\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Tests that detect genes linked to hereditary conditions increasing pancreatic cancer risk.<SEP>Tests that analyze genes to detect cancer risk or presence.<SEP>Tests to identify genetic mutations related to cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683069}, {\"entity_name\": \"Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of pancreatic cancer that is inherited through genes, accounting for about 1 in 10 cases.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683069}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Genetic Test\", \"tgt_id\": \"Hereditary Condition\", \"description\": \"Genetic tests can identify hereditary conditions that increase adrenal tumor risk.\", \"keywords\": \"detects\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Breast Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Hereditary Genetic Testing\", \"description\": \"Depending on family history, breast cancer patients may be referred for hereditary genetic testing.\", \"keywords\": \"genetic testing,hereditary risk\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Tumor\", \"tgt_id\": \"Hereditary Condition\", \"description\": \"Hereditary conditions are linked to an increased risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"increases risk of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Familial Adenomatous Polyposis\", \"description\": \"Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a factor that increases the risk of carrying certain germline mutations, leading to a recommendation for germline testing in prostate cancer patients.<SEP>Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a factor that may prompt inherited gene testing for prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"ancestry,genetic risk,hereditary,risk\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Genetic Testing\", \"description\": \"Genetic testing may be done if family history suggests inherited risk.<SEP>Genetic testing may be performed based on family history to assess cancer risk.<SEP>Genetic testing is part of assessing family history of cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer risk,hereditary,related,related to,risk assessment\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Tests\", \"tgt_id\": \"Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer\", \"description\": \"Genetic tests detect genes linked to hereditary conditions that increase pancreatic cancer risk.\", \"keywords\": \"detection,diagnosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Risk Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Kidney Cancer\", \"description\": \"Genetic risk testing may be used to assess inherited risk for kidney cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"risk assessment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome\", \"description\": \"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2\", \"description\": \"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1\", \"description\": \"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Genetic Test\", \"description\": \"Family history of cancer may prompt genetic testing.\", \"keywords\": \"indication,testing\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic cancer risk testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Germline mutations\", \"description\": \"Genetic cancer risk testing looks for germline mutations inherited from parents.\", \"keywords\": \"detects,genetic\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Cancer Risk Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Treatment Plan\", \"description\": \"Genetic testing results can influence treatment and risk management.\", \"keywords\": \"informs,risk\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Test\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Genetic tests are used to assess risk and guide prostate cancer care.<SEP>Genetic tests are used to find mutations that may increase the risk of prostate cancer.<SEP>Genetic tests are used to find abnormal changes in genes that may be related to prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"genetic testing,management,mutations,risk assessment\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Li-Fraumeni Syndrome\", \"description\": \"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Counselor\", \"tgt_id\": \"Germline Testing\", \"description\": \"Patients may be referred to a genetic counselor for germline testing to assess inherited cancer risk.\", \"keywords\": \"inherited risk,referral\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022\", \"description\": \"The guidelines discuss the importance of family health history in the context of adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"guidance,health information\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683094}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome\", \"description\": \"Hereditary paraganglioma-pheochromocytoma syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Carney Complex\", \"description\": \"Carney complex is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Neurofibromatosis Type 1\", \"description\": \"Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Throat Cancer\", \"description\": \"Family history is considered to assess potential genetic risks, though head and neck cancers are not typically inherited.\", \"keywords\": \"risk assessment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Bone Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Genetic Cancer Risk Testing\", \"description\": \"Genetic cancer risk testing may be used to assess inherited risk for bone cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"risk assessment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"BRCA Genes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Mutations in BRCA genes are important biomarkers for ovarian cancer treatment planning.<SEP>Mutations in BRCA genes are the most common cause of hereditary ovarian cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"biomarker,genetic mutation,hereditary risk,mutation,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Risk Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Heart Tests\", \"description\": \"Heart tests and genetic risk testing are part of testing for ALL.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnostic procedures\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lynch Syndrome\", \"description\": \"Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Adrenal Gland Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome\", \"description\": \"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Genetic testing can be done to find specific genetic changes linked with prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"detection,genetic risk\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Family Health History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Genetic Risk Testing\", \"description\": \"Family health history may inform the need for genetic risk testing.\", \"keywords\": \"informs\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"BRCA Genes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Genetic Testing\", \"description\": \"Genetic testing of blood is recommended to detect inherited BRCA mutations in ovarian cancer patients.\", \"keywords\": \"inherited risk,mutation detection\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Genetic Risk Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Testing for Stomach Cancer\", \"description\": \"Genetic risk testing may be included in the testing process for stomach cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnostic procedure\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Germline Mutation\", \"description\": \"A family history of cancer may indicate the presence of germline mutations.\", \"keywords\": \"genetic testing,risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Genetic Test", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A test used to identify genetic mutations associated with hereditary conditions.<SEP>A test to identify abnormal changes in genes, used to assess cancer risk and guide treatment.<SEP>A test to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes, including germline testing.<SEP>A test used to find harmful changes in genes.<SEP>A test to identify mutations in genes.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682801}, {"entity_name": "Hereditary Condition", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A genetic mutation passed from parent to child that can increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682801}, {"entity_name": "Breast Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Breast Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in breast tissue, most commonly originating in the epithelial cells of the terminal duct lobular units, specifically within the ducts or lobules. The vast majority of breast cancers are carcinomas. This malignant disease can spread to surrounding tissue and other parts of the body, such as the lungs; when it metastasizes, it is still referred to as breast cancer.\n\nThe condition is frequently associated with germline mutations, particularly in the BRCA genes, and can be hereditary in approximately 1 out of 10 cases. A personal or family history of breast cancer may also be linked to an increased risk of developing other cancers, such as prostate cancer and thyroid cancer. Clinical discussions of breast cancer often focus on staging, prognostic factors, and the use of biomarker testing and tumor markers for diagnosis and management.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682946}, {"entity_name": "Hereditary Genetic Testing", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Testing for inherited gene mutations using blood or saliva; also referred to as genetic risk testing.<SEP>Testing to identify inherited genetic mutations that increase the risk of cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683079}, {"entity_name": "Adrenal Tumor", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor that can be found in one or both adrenal glands; most are benign, but some can be cancerous.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682801}, {"entity_name": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A rare cancer that starts in one or both adrenal glands, affecting approximately 1 to 2 people per million.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682796}, {"entity_name": "Familial Adenomatous Polyposis", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A hereditary condition that may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor.<SEP>An inherited syndrome causing numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, often leading to colon cancer.<SEP>An inherited syndrome related to colon cancer.<SEP>An inherited syndrome related to thyroid cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682796}, {"entity_name": "Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "An ethnic background associated with higher risk of certain genetic mutations, including BRCA1 and BRCA2.<SEP>An ethnic background associated with higher risk of certain genetic mutations.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683079}, {"entity_name": "Prostate Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Prostate Cancer is a disease in which cells in the prostate gland grow out of control, and it is one of the most common types of cancer. The condition can be early-stage (localized) or advanced-stage (metastatic), with the latter occurring when cancer spreads outside the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes and bones, through a process called metastasis. Prostate cancer is often slow-growing and asymptomatic in its early stages, contributing to high survival rates when detected early. Risk factors for developing the disease include age, family history, genetic factors, race, and lifestyle. Detection and risk assessment involve various tests, including PSA (prostate-specific antigen) levels, biopsy, and grading, and the disease often requires staging and treatment. The condition is also a focus of risk assessment and genetic testing discussions, as it is characterized by the presence of prostate cancer cells and abnormal cell growth within the gland.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683075}, {"entity_name": "Family History", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Family History is a comprehensive record of the health information of an individual's blood relatives, including parents, siblings, children, grandparents, aunts, uncles, nieces, nephews, and half-siblings. This information encompasses the presence of various diseases and health conditions, such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes, along with the age at which relatives were diagnosed. It serves as a critical tool in clinical practice, used to assess an individual's risk for inherited conditions and to guide healthcare decisions, treatment plans, and preventive care.\n\nThe utility of Family History is particularly pronounced in oncology. It is a key risk factor for several types of cancer, including basal cell skin cancer, prostate cancer, thyroid cancer, and bladder cancer. For instance, having a close relative (like a parent or sibling) with prostate or bladder cancer increases an individual's chance of developing the disease. Furthermore, a detailed history of cancer occurrences in family members, including the specific type of cancer and age of onset, is essential for identifying potential hereditary cancer syndromes. This information can prompt healthcare providers to recommend genetic testing and risk assessment to determine if an individual carries a higher genetic predisposition for certain malignancies, such as chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682799}, {"entity_name": "Genetic Testing", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Genetic Testing is a medical diagnostic process that analyzes DNA to identify inherited (germline) mutations, typically performed on normal tissue samples such as blood, saliva, cheek swabs, or urine. This form of testing is distinct from tumor mutation testing, as it focuses on determining whether an individual carries hereditary genetic changes that increase their risk for developing cancer. It is commonly used to detect mutations in BRCA genes and other cancer-related genes, and is recommended for all individuals diagnosed with ovarian cancer to guide clinical management.\n\nThe scope of genetic testing extends beyond hereditary risk assessment. In oncology, it is also utilized to analyze genetic changes and biomarkers within cancer cells—such as leukemia cells—to inform treatment decisions and prognosis. Additionally, genetic testing serves as a method to identify specific mutations linked to prostate cancer and other malignancies, helping to tailor therapeutic approaches. The test can be performed on various sample types, including blood, saliva, urine, or tumor tissue, depending on the clinical question.\n\nGenetic testing is also valuable for identifying mutations that cause inherited cancer syndromes, and results are often relevant for family members, as knowing whether a relative has undergone testing can influence interpretation of one’s own results. Overall, genetic testing plays a critical role in cancer risk assessment, early detection, and personalized treatment planning across multiple cancer types.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683022}, {"entity_name": "Genetic Tests", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Tests that detect genes linked to hereditary conditions increasing pancreatic cancer risk.<SEP>Tests that analyze genes to detect cancer risk or presence.<SEP>Tests to identify genetic mutations related to cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683069}, {"entity_name": "Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of pancreatic cancer that is inherited through genes, accounting for about 1 in 10 cases.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683069}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Genetic Test", "tgt_id": "Hereditary Condition", "description": "Genetic tests can identify hereditary conditions that increase adrenal tumor risk.", "keywords": "detects", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Breast Cancer", "tgt_id": "Hereditary Genetic Testing", "description": "Depending on family history, breast cancer patients may be referred for hereditary genetic testing.", "keywords": "genetic testing,hereditary risk", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Tumor", "tgt_id": "Hereditary Condition", "description": "Hereditary conditions are linked to an increased risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "increases risk of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Familial Adenomatous Polyposis", "description": "Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a factor that increases the risk of carrying certain germline mutations, leading to a recommendation for germline testing in prostate cancer patients.<SEP>Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a factor that may prompt inherited gene testing for prostate cancer.", "keywords": "ancestry,genetic risk,hereditary,risk", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Genetic Testing", "description": "Genetic testing may be done if family history suggests inherited risk.<SEP>Genetic testing may be performed based on family history to assess cancer risk.<SEP>Genetic testing is part of assessing family history of cancer.", "keywords": "cancer risk,hereditary,related,related to,risk assessment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Genetic Tests", "tgt_id": "Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer", "description": "Genetic tests detect genes linked to hereditary conditions that increase pancreatic cancer risk.", "keywords": "detection,diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Genetic Risk Testing", "tgt_id": "Kidney Cancer", "description": "Genetic risk testing may be used to assess inherited risk for kidney cancer.", "keywords": "risk assessment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome", "description": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2", "description": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1", "description": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Genetic Test", "description": "Family history of cancer may prompt genetic testing.", "keywords": "indication,testing", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Genetic cancer risk testing", "tgt_id": "Germline mutations", "description": "Genetic cancer risk testing looks for germline mutations inherited from parents.", "keywords": "detects,genetic", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Genetic Cancer Risk Testing", "tgt_id": "Treatment Plan", "description": "Genetic testing results can influence treatment and risk management.", "keywords": "informs,risk", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Genetic Test", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Genetic tests are used to assess risk and guide prostate cancer care.<SEP>Genetic tests are used to find mutations that may increase the risk of prostate cancer.<SEP>Genetic tests are used to find abnormal changes in genes that may be related to prostate cancer.", "keywords": "genetic testing,management,mutations,risk assessment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Li-Fraumeni Syndrome", "description": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Genetic Counselor", "tgt_id": "Germline Testing", "description": "Patients may be referred to a genetic counselor for germline testing to assess inherited cancer risk.", "keywords": "inherited risk,referral", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022", "description": "The guidelines discuss the importance of family health history in the context of adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "guidance,health information", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683094}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome", "description": "Hereditary paraganglioma-pheochromocytoma syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Carney Complex", "description": "Carney complex is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Neurofibromatosis Type 1", "description": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Throat Cancer", "description": "Family history is considered to assess potential genetic risks, though head and neck cancers are not typically inherited.", "keywords": "risk assessment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Bone Cancer", "tgt_id": "Genetic Cancer Risk Testing", "description": "Genetic cancer risk testing may be used to assess inherited risk for bone cancer.", "keywords": "risk assessment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "BRCA Genes", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Mutations in BRCA genes are important biomarkers for ovarian cancer treatment planning.<SEP>Mutations in BRCA genes are the most common cause of hereditary ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "biomarker,genetic mutation,hereditary risk,mutation,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Genetic Risk Testing", "tgt_id": "Heart Tests", "description": "Heart tests and genetic risk testing are part of testing for ALL.", "keywords": "diagnostic procedures", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Lynch Syndrome", "description": "Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Adrenal Gland Cancer", "tgt_id": "Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome", "description": "Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Genetic Testing", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Genetic testing can be done to find specific genetic changes linked with prostate cancer.", "keywords": "detection,genetic risk", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Family Health History", "tgt_id": "Genetic Risk Testing", "description": "Family health history may inform the need for genetic risk testing.", "keywords": "informs", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "BRCA Genes", "tgt_id": "Genetic Testing", "description": "Genetic testing of blood is recommended to detect inherited BRCA mutations in ovarian cancer patients.", "keywords": "inherited risk,mutation detection", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Genetic Risk Testing", "tgt_id": "Testing for Stomach Cancer", "description": "Genetic risk testing may be included in the testing process for stomach cancer.", "keywords": "diagnostic procedure", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Germline Mutation", "description": "A family history of cancer may indicate the presence of germline mutations.", "keywords": "genetic testing,risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5807, "output_tokens": 75, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1416.1058999598026, "generation_time_ms": 1152.258999994956, "total_time_ms": 2569.1747000673786, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["genetic tests", "hereditary risk factors", "adrenal tumors"], "low_level": []}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 50, "total_relations_found": 40, "entities_after_truncation": 13, "relations_after_truncation": 32, "merged_chunks_count": 41, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "Which genetic tests may be performed to identify hereditary risk factors for adrenal tumors?", "question_type": "Fact Retrieval", "ground_truth": "Genetic testing may be performed to identify hereditary risk factors.", "evidence": "Genetic testing may be performed to identify hereditary risk factors for adrenal tumors.", "evidence_relations": "Genetic testing may be performed to identify hereditary risk factors", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-5a6b98a7", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "I don't know.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Numbered Stages\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Numbered stages range from stage 0 to stage 4, with 4 being the most advanced, based on TNM scores.<SEP>Stages from 1 to 4 indicating the extent of cancer, with 4 being most advanced.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, which connects the back of the nose to the back of the mouth.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, the area connecting the back of the nose to the back of the mouth.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that starts in the nasopharynx, the upper part of the throat behind the nose.<SEP>A cancer that starts in the nasopharynx, specifically squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Advanced-Stage Disease\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A later stage of cancer, more common in mediastinal gray zone lymphoma compared to other types.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Testing For Npc\", \"entity_type\": \"event\", \"description\": \"The process of undergoing various tests to diagnose and plan treatment for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Phases of Treatment\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The stages of cancer treatment, including induction and consolidation, as mentioned in the text.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Nasopharynx\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"The upper part of the throat near the nasal cavity, one of the three parts of the pharynx.<SEP>A small, tube-shaped structure above the soft palate that connects the nasal cavity to the oropharynx.<SEP>The upper part of the throat behind the nose, connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx.<SEP>The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.<SEP>The upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683056}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stage IV\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Metastatic breast cancer stage; cancer has spread to distant sites.<SEP>The most advanced stage of cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683080}, {\"entity_name\": \"Pathologic Stage\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Pathologic Stage, also known as surgical stage or pathologic stage (p), is the stage of cancer determined by examining tissue removed during a surgical procedure. This assessment is performed after the surgery has taken place, distinguishing it from clinical staging, which is based on examinations conducted before treatment. The pathologic stage is widely regarded as providing the most accurate picture of the disease's extent, as it is based on direct analysis of the actual removed tissue rather than imaging or physical exams alone.\\n\\nThis staging method offers more precise and detailed information regarding key prognostic factors, particularly the size of the primary tumor and the status of lymph nodes. By evaluating the tissue excised during operations such as lymph node removal, physicians can determine the exact degree of cancer spread. Consequently, the pathologic stage serves as a critical prognostic stage, informing treatment decisions and providing a more reliable basis for predicting patient outcomes than other staging methods.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683031}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stage 1 Cervical Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"An early stage of cervical cancer, including stage 1B3, with specific follow-up imaging recommendations.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Clinical Stage\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Clinical Stage (c) is a rating or classification of cancer assigned before any treatment is administered. It is determined through a combination of physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests, such as X-rays or scans, and serves as a pre-treatment estimate of how far the cancer has spread. This staging is based on non-surgical findings, meaning it does not incorporate information obtained from surgical procedures. It is also considered a prognostic stage, used to guide initial treatment decisions and predict outcomes. The clinical stage is established prior to surgery and relies on the results of physical examinations and imaging, without including pathological data from operative findings.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683028}, {\"entity_name\": \"Prognostic Stage\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A staging system that combines anatomic TNM with tumor grade and biomarker status to predict outcome.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683080}, {\"entity_name\": \"Head And Neck Cancers\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area of the body.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683082}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stage III\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Invasive breast cancer stage with more extensive lymph node involvement.<SEP>A more advanced stage of cancer than stage II.<SEP>A stage of anal cancer that is nonmetastatic and treated with chemoradiation.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683080}, {\"entity_name\": \"Cancer Treatment\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Treatments such as radiation therapy and chemotherapy that can have side effects on fertility and other body functions.<SEP>Medical interventions such as chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery used to treat cancer, which may affect fertility.<SEP>Therapies for cancer that may cause bone damage, as mentioned in the context of bone scans.<SEP>Medical interventions such as chemotherapy that can affect fertility and require precautions during treatment.<SEP>General term for therapies used to treat cancer, including targeted therapies and immunotherapies.<SEP>Treatment based on tumor location, including various modalities such as surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy.<SEP>Medical interventions to treat cancer, which may be affected by smoking.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682996}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stage\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The extent of cancer spread, determined by grade and other factors.<SEP>A rating of the extent of cancer before treatment, used to determine treatment options.<SEP>A measure of how far cancer has grown or spread, used to predict disease course and plan treatment.<SEP>A rating system used to describe the physical extent of cancer, indicating how far it has grown into the bladder wall and spread to other parts of the body. Stages range from 0 to 4.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682976}, {\"entity_name\": \"Head and Neck Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Cancers of the head and neck region, linked to smoking and vaping.<SEP>Cancers occurring in the head and neck region, often affecting hearing and treatment.<SEP>A type of cancer that occurs in the head and neck region, which can affect jaw, teeth, and gums health.<SEP>A group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area, including cancers of the oral cavity, oropharynx, nasal cavity, nasopharynx, larynx, and hypopharynx.<SEP>Cancers that occur in the head and neck region, including nasopharyngeal cancer.<SEP>A type of cancer caused by HPV.<SEP>A type of cancer caused by HPV, affecting the head and neck region.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683013}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Clinical Stage\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Clinical stage is a best guess of cancer spread based on testing, used for treatment planning.\", \"keywords\": \"prognosis,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Testing For Npc\", \"description\": \"Testing for NPC is used to diagnose and plan treatment for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnoses,plans treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Pathologic Stage\", \"description\": \"The pathologic stage of ovarian cancer is determined after surgery and guides treatment.\", \"keywords\": \"post-surgery,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharynx\", \"description\": \"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a cancer that affects the nasopharynx.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma starts in the nasopharynx.\", \"keywords\": \"affects,location,originates in,site of origin\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Cancer Stage\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Cancer stage describes where ovarian cancer has spread.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,prognosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Pathologic Stage\", \"description\": \"Pathologic stage is determined after surgery by examining tissue removed during surgery for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"keywords\": \"determined after surgery\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Surgical Staging\", \"description\": \"Surgical staging is the most accurate way to stage ovarian cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"accuracy,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Clinical Stage\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Clinical stage is assigned to nasopharyngeal carcinoma before any treatment based on imaging and biopsy.\", \"keywords\": \"assigned before treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 4\", \"description\": \"Ovarian cancer can be classified as Stage 4 when cancer has spread to distant sites.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Head And Neck Cancers\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a type of head and neck cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"type of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 3A1\", \"description\": \"Ovarian cancer can be classified as Stage 3A1 when cancer has spread to retroperitoneal lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cancer Treatment\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Cancer treatment is used to treat nasopharyngeal carcinoma, based on tumor location.\", \"keywords\": \"treats\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Pathologic Stage\", \"description\": \"Pathologic stage is determined after surgery by examining removed tissue.\", \"keywords\": \"determination,staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Head and Neck Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer type,includes\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Cancer Stage\", \"tgt_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"description\": \"Treatment for cervical cancer is based on the cancer stage.\", \"keywords\": \"determines treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Epstein-Barr Virus\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Epstein-Barr virus is associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Epstein-Barr virus levels in the blood are used in prognostic staging for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Epstein-Barr virus is found in neck lymph nodes in cases of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"keywords\": \"associated with,prognostic factor\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Surgical Staging\", \"description\": \"Surgical staging is used to determine the extent of cervical cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"staging,surgery\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Head And Neck Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Cancer\", \"description\": \"Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,type of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 2 Cervical Cancer\", \"description\": \"Stage 2 cervical cancer has grown beyond the cervix.\", \"keywords\": \"stage classification\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Health Care Provider\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Health care providers evaluate and treat patients with nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"keywords\": \"treats\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 1A2\", \"description\": \"Stage 1A2 is a specific stage of cervical cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,stage\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Biomarker Testing\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Biomarker testing is used to identify genes or mutations in nasopharyngeal carcinoma that might suggest treatment.\", \"keywords\": \"used for treatment decisions\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 3\", \"description\": \"Stage 3 is a specific stage of cervical cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,stage\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"NCCN Patient Guidelines\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Cancer\", \"description\": \"More information on nasopharyngeal cancer can be found at NCCN.org/patientguidelines.\", \"keywords\": \"information available at\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Early-Stage Cancer\", \"description\": \"Early-stage cervical cancer is often treated with surgery.<SEP>Early-stage cancer refers to cervical cancer confined to the cervix.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,stage,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Family history is considered in assessing risk for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Pre-Treatment Surgical Staging\", \"description\": \"Pre-treatment surgical staging is used to determine if cancer has spread to lymph nodes before treatment.\", \"keywords\": \"staging\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Carcinoma\", \"description\": \"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma for which the TNM staging information is used.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is the type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma being staged.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,type of\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 4A Cervical Cancer\", \"description\": \"Stage 4A cervical cancer has spread to nearby organs such as the bladder or rectum.\", \"keywords\": \"stage classification\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Eye Exam\", \"tgt_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Cancer\", \"description\": \"An eye exam might be recommended before treatment for nasopharyngeal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"examination,recommendation\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stage 4A\", \"description\": \"Stage 4A is a specific stage of cervical cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"classification,stage\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Numbered Stages", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Numbered stages range from stage 0 to stage 4, with 4 being the most advanced, based on TNM scores.<SEP>Stages from 1 to 4 indicating the extent of cancer, with 4 being most advanced.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, which connects the back of the nose to the back of the mouth.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, the area connecting the back of the nose to the back of the mouth.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that starts in the nasopharynx, the upper part of the throat behind the nose.<SEP>A cancer that starts in the nasopharynx, specifically squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Advanced-Stage Disease", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A later stage of cancer, more common in mediastinal gray zone lymphoma compared to other types.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Testing For Npc", "entity_type": "event", "description": "The process of undergoing various tests to diagnose and plan treatment for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Phases of Treatment", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The stages of cancer treatment, including induction and consolidation, as mentioned in the text.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Nasopharynx", "entity_type": "location", "description": "The upper part of the throat near the nasal cavity, one of the three parts of the pharynx.<SEP>A small, tube-shaped structure above the soft palate that connects the nasal cavity to the oropharynx.<SEP>The upper part of the throat behind the nose, connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx.<SEP>The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.<SEP>The upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683056}, {"entity_name": "Stage IV", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Metastatic breast cancer stage; cancer has spread to distant sites.<SEP>The most advanced stage of cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683080}, {"entity_name": "Pathologic Stage", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Pathologic Stage, also known as surgical stage or pathologic stage (p), is the stage of cancer determined by examining tissue removed during a surgical procedure. This assessment is performed after the surgery has taken place, distinguishing it from clinical staging, which is based on examinations conducted before treatment. The pathologic stage is widely regarded as providing the most accurate picture of the disease's extent, as it is based on direct analysis of the actual removed tissue rather than imaging or physical exams alone.\n\nThis staging method offers more precise and detailed information regarding key prognostic factors, particularly the size of the primary tumor and the status of lymph nodes. By evaluating the tissue excised during operations such as lymph node removal, physicians can determine the exact degree of cancer spread. Consequently, the pathologic stage serves as a critical prognostic stage, informing treatment decisions and providing a more reliable basis for predicting patient outcomes than other staging methods.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683031}, {"entity_name": "Stage 1 Cervical Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "An early stage of cervical cancer, including stage 1B3, with specific follow-up imaging recommendations.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Clinical Stage", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Clinical Stage (c) is a rating or classification of cancer assigned before any treatment is administered. It is determined through a combination of physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests, such as X-rays or scans, and serves as a pre-treatment estimate of how far the cancer has spread. This staging is based on non-surgical findings, meaning it does not incorporate information obtained from surgical procedures. It is also considered a prognostic stage, used to guide initial treatment decisions and predict outcomes. The clinical stage is established prior to surgery and relies on the results of physical examinations and imaging, without including pathological data from operative findings.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683028}, {"entity_name": "Prognostic Stage", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A staging system that combines anatomic TNM with tumor grade and biomarker status to predict outcome.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683080}, {"entity_name": "Head And Neck Cancers", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area of the body.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683082}, {"entity_name": "Stage III", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Invasive breast cancer stage with more extensive lymph node involvement.<SEP>A more advanced stage of cancer than stage II.<SEP>A stage of anal cancer that is nonmetastatic and treated with chemoradiation.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683080}, {"entity_name": "Cancer Treatment", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Treatments such as radiation therapy and chemotherapy that can have side effects on fertility and other body functions.<SEP>Medical interventions such as chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery used to treat cancer, which may affect fertility.<SEP>Therapies for cancer that may cause bone damage, as mentioned in the context of bone scans.<SEP>Medical interventions such as chemotherapy that can affect fertility and require precautions during treatment.<SEP>General term for therapies used to treat cancer, including targeted therapies and immunotherapies.<SEP>Treatment based on tumor location, including various modalities such as surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy.<SEP>Medical interventions to treat cancer, which may be affected by smoking.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682996}, {"entity_name": "Stage", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The extent of cancer spread, determined by grade and other factors.<SEP>A rating of the extent of cancer before treatment, used to determine treatment options.<SEP>A measure of how far cancer has grown or spread, used to predict disease course and plan treatment.<SEP>A rating system used to describe the physical extent of cancer, indicating how far it has grown into the bladder wall and spread to other parts of the body. Stages range from 0 to 4.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682976}, {"entity_name": "Head and Neck Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Cancers of the head and neck region, linked to smoking and vaping.<SEP>Cancers occurring in the head and neck region, often affecting hearing and treatment.<SEP>A type of cancer that occurs in the head and neck region, which can affect jaw, teeth, and gums health.<SEP>A group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area, including cancers of the oral cavity, oropharynx, nasal cavity, nasopharynx, larynx, and hypopharynx.<SEP>Cancers that occur in the head and neck region, including nasopharyngeal cancer.<SEP>A type of cancer caused by HPV.<SEP>A type of cancer caused by HPV, affecting the head and neck region.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683013}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Clinical Stage", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Clinical stage is a best guess of cancer spread based on testing, used for treatment planning.", "keywords": "prognosis,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Testing For Npc", "description": "Testing for NPC is used to diagnose and plan treatment for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "keywords": "diagnoses,plans treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "tgt_id": "Pathologic Stage", "description": "The pathologic stage of ovarian cancer is determined after surgery and guides treatment.", "keywords": "post-surgery,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Nasopharynx", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a cancer that affects the nasopharynx.<SEP>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma starts in the nasopharynx.", "keywords": "affects,location,originates in,site of origin", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Cancer Stage", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Cancer stage describes where ovarian cancer has spread.", "keywords": "classification,prognosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Pathologic Stage", "description": "Pathologic stage is determined after surgery by examining tissue removed during surgery for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "keywords": "determined after surgery", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgical Staging", "description": "Surgical staging is the most accurate way to stage ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "accuracy,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Clinical Stage", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Clinical stage is assigned to nasopharyngeal carcinoma before any treatment based on imaging and biopsy.", "keywords": "assigned before treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 4", "description": "Ovarian cancer can be classified as Stage 4 when cancer has spread to distant sites.", "keywords": "classification,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Head And Neck Cancers", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a type of head and neck cancer.", "keywords": "type of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 3A1", "description": "Ovarian cancer can be classified as Stage 3A1 when cancer has spread to retroperitoneal lymph nodes.", "keywords": "classification,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cancer Treatment", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Cancer treatment is used to treat nasopharyngeal carcinoma, based on tumor location.", "keywords": "treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Pathologic Stage", "description": "Pathologic stage is determined after surgery by examining removed tissue.", "keywords": "determination,staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Head and Neck Cancer", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.", "keywords": "cancer type,includes", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Cancer Stage", "tgt_id": "Cervical Cancer", "description": "Treatment for cervical cancer is based on the cancer stage.", "keywords": "determines treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Epstein-Barr Virus", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Epstein-Barr virus is associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Epstein-Barr virus levels in the blood are used in prognostic staging for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Epstein-Barr virus is found in neck lymph nodes in cases of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "keywords": "associated with,prognostic factor", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgical Staging", "description": "Surgical staging is used to determine the extent of cervical cancer.", "keywords": "staging,surgery", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Head And Neck Cancer", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Cancer", "description": "Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer.", "keywords": "cancer,type of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 2 Cervical Cancer", "description": "Stage 2 cervical cancer has grown beyond the cervix.", "keywords": "stage classification", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Health Care Provider", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Health care providers evaluate and treat patients with nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "keywords": "treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 1A2", "description": "Stage 1A2 is a specific stage of cervical cancer.", "keywords": "classification,stage", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Biomarker Testing", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Biomarker testing is used to identify genes or mutations in nasopharyngeal carcinoma that might suggest treatment.", "keywords": "used for treatment decisions", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 3", "description": "Stage 3 is a specific stage of cervical cancer.", "keywords": "classification,stage", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "NCCN Patient Guidelines", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Cancer", "description": "More information on nasopharyngeal cancer can be found at NCCN.org/patientguidelines.", "keywords": "information available at", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Early-Stage Cancer", "description": "Early-stage cervical cancer is often treated with surgery.<SEP>Early-stage cancer refers to cervical cancer confined to the cervix.", "keywords": "classification,stage,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "description": "Family history is considered in assessing risk for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Pre-Treatment Surgical Staging", "description": "Pre-treatment surgical staging is used to determine if cancer has spread to lymph nodes before treatment.", "keywords": "staging", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "description": "Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma for which the TNM staging information is used.<SEP>Squamous cell carcinoma is the type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma being staged.", "keywords": "cancer,type of", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 4A Cervical Cancer", "description": "Stage 4A cervical cancer has spread to nearby organs such as the bladder or rectum.", "keywords": "stage classification", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Eye Exam", "tgt_id": "Nasopharyngeal Cancer", "description": "An eye exam might be recommended before treatment for nasopharyngeal cancer.", "keywords": "examination,recommendation", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Stage 4A", "description": "Stage 4A is a specific stage of cervical cancer.", "keywords": "classification,stage", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5747, "output_tokens": 39, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1852.5096999946982, "generation_time_ms": 912.0005000149831, "total_time_ms": 2765.1850000256673, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["symptoms", "diagnostic process", "nasopharyngeal carcinoma"], "low_level": ["advanced stages"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 82, "total_relations_found": 150, "entities_after_truncation": 16, "relations_after_truncation": 31, "merged_chunks_count": 42, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "Why might symptoms of nasopharyngeal carcinoma be absent until advanced stages, and how does this affect the diagnostic process?", "question_type": "Complex Reasoning", "ground_truth": "Symptoms may be absent until advanced stage, making imaging and biopsy essential for early diagnosis.", "evidence": "Symptoms of nasopharyngeal carcinoma may be absent until advanced stages.; The absence of symptoms until advanced stages makes imaging essential for early diagnosis.; The absence of symptoms until advanced stages makes biopsy essential for early diagnosis.", "evidence_relations": "Symptoms of NPC may be absent until advanced stage and can be similar to other conditions; Diagnosis of NPC involves imaging (CT, MRI, PET/CT, ultrasound) and biopsy", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-09eac393", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Radioactive iodine therapy is not used for medullary thyroid cancer because this type of thyroid cancer does not take up iodine, making RAI therapy ineffective. Alternative therapies recommended include targeted therapy (such as kinase inhibitors) for cases that cannot be treated with surgery, have recurred, or have metastasized.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Radioactive Iodine Therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A treatment using radioactive iodine to kill thyroid cancer cells left in the body after surgery.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"RAI Therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Radioactive iodine therapy, a treatment for thyroid cancer, mentioned as a standard treatment that targeted therapy is used when RAI therapy cannot be performed.<SEP>Radioactive iodine therapy used to destroy remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells after surgery.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Total Thyroidectomy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A surgical procedure that removes the entire thyroid gland, often with nearby lymph nodes if cancer is suspected.<SEP>Surgical removal of the entire thyroid gland.<SEP>Surgical removal of the entire thyroid, recommended for suspected follicular or oncocytic tumors that have grown beyond the thyroid or are larger than 4 cm.<SEP>A surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, often used to treat thyroid cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Medullary Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The third most common type of thyroid cancer, which can be inherited and is the focus of a specific chapter.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that does not take up iodine and is not effectively treated with RAI therapy.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that may have RET gene fusion.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Differentiated Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of thyroid cancer that takes up iodine and is often treated with RAI therapy after thyroidectomy.<SEP>A group of thyroid cancers that arise from follicular cells and retain some normal thyroid tissue features.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"A treatment using iodine-131 to selectively kill thyroid cancer cells, administered orally, often after thyroidectomy for high-risk cancers.<SEP>A treatment that uses radioactive iodine to destroy remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Papillary Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"disease\", \"description\": \"The most common type of thyroid cancer, often slow-growing.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of cancer that forms in the thyroid gland, with several types, most curable with appropriate treatment.<SEP>Cancer that forms in the thyroid gland, with several types including papillary, follicular, oncocytic, medullary, and anaplastic.<SEP>A disease characterized by malignant cells in the thyroid gland, with different types including differentiated, medullary, and anaplastic.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the thyroid gland. Targeted therapies are used for certain thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI therapy, have returned, or have metastasized.<SEP>A cancer that forms in the tissues of the thyroid gland.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the thyroid gland, with metastatic forms that may require targeted therapy or monitoring.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Oncocytic Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Iodine-131\", \"entity_type\": \"naturalobject\", \"description\": \"A radioactive isotope of iodine used in RAI therapy and whole-body scans.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Thyroidectomy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Surgical removal of the thyroid gland, often performed before RAI therapy for thyroid cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Whole-Body RAI Imaging\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Imaging test to look for remaining thyroid cancer cells after RAI therapy.<SEP>Imaging after radioactive iodine therapy to detect remaining cancer cells.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Follicular Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of thyroid cancer that can be caused by Cowden syndrome.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Metastatic Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Metastatic Cancer is a term used to describe cancer that has spread from its original (primary) site to other, distant parts of the body. This process of spread can involve various organs and tissues, with common distant sites including the lungs, liver, bones, and brain, as well as distant lymph nodes. The condition is often characterized as very advanced disease, and when cancer is identified as stage 4 at the time of diagnosis, it is considered metastatic from the outset. In specific contexts, such as esophageal cancer, the metastatic stage specifically denotes that the disease has spread to distant bodily locations. Regardless of the primary tumor's origin, the defining feature of metastatic cancer is its presence in areas far removed from the initial site of development.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682934}, {\"entity_name\": \"Whole-Body Radioiodine Scan\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"An imaging procedure performed after RAI therapy to detect remaining thyroid tissue or hidden cancer areas.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Metastatic Thyroid Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"UNKNOWN\", \"description\": \"Joining a clinical trial is strongly encouraged for everyone with metastatic thyroid cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"entity_name\": \"Targeted Therapy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Targeted Therapy is a form of systemic cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to identify and attack specific molecules, genes, proteins, or receptors involved in the growth, spread, and survival of cancer cells. Unlike broad-spectrum treatments like chemotherapy, this approach focuses on particular genetic changes, mutations (e.g., PIK3CA), or biomarkers (e.g., PARP inhibitors) present in tumors, making it effective for cancers with certain molecular features. By targeting these specific parts of cancer cells, the therapy aims to slow or stop tumor progression, and it may potentially cause fewer side effects than chemotherapy, though it can also be combined with chemotherapy in certain contexts, such as for primary CNS lymphoma.\\n\\nThis therapy is applicable across various cancer types, including advanced-stage prostate cancer, recurrent and/or metastatic cervical cancer, and thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy, have recurred, or have metastasized and continue to grow. For thyroid cancer, the targeted therapies currently used are kinase inhibitors, typically administered as swallowed capsules. However, the treatment is not without risks; side effects can include body aches, rash, high blood pressure, and abnormal bleeding, and some agents may adversely affect the heart, skin, and digestive system. Additionally, targeted therapy can impact fertility, and its use is often guided by biomarker testing to determine patient eligibility.\\n\\nAs a systemic therapy, it is generally reserved for cases where specific genetic targets are identified, and it may be employed when other treatments have failed or are unsuitable. The overarching goal remains to attack specific types of cancer cells while minimizing harm to normal cells, thereby offering a more precise treatment option for individuals with particular gene mutations or molecular profiles.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682881}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Advanced prostate cancer can be treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"non-hormone,treated by,treatment\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine Therapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Total Thyroidectomy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy to kill any remaining thyroid cancer cells.\", \"keywords\": \"adjuvant treatment,residual cancer\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy may be needed before surgery if ovarian cancer is advanced.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat ovarian cancer, sometimes before surgery.<SEP>Chemotherapy may be given first to shrink ovarian cancer if surgery is not initially an option.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method used for ovarian cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat ovarian cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"neoadjuvant,shrink tumor,systemic therapy,treatment\", \"weight\": 6.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Differentiated Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"description\": \"Differentiated thyroid cancers that take up iodine are often treated with RAI therapy after total thyroidectomy.\", \"keywords\": \"responds,treated with\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is used to treat cervical cancer, often in combination with radiation.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Thyroid Cancer\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is used to treat thyroid cancer, especially differentiated types that take up iodine, to lower recurrence risk or treat spread.<SEP>RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy to kill remaining cancer cells.\", \"keywords\": \"targets,treated with,treats\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer can be treated with radiation therapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment option for prostate cancer, often used for higher-risk cases.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"management,non-hormone,treated by,treatment\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Medullary Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is not effective against medullary thyroid cancer because it does not take up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"not effective,resistant\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer and as part of chemoradiation.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation therapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment,treats\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Total Thyroidectomy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy.\", \"keywords\": \"may be followed by\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy is a treatment for cervical cancer using high-energy x-rays.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for cervical cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"radiation,treatment,treatment for\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Iodine-131\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy uses iodine-131 to selectively kill thyroid cancer cells that take up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"radioactive isotope,uses\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Bladder Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, either directly into the bladder or systemically for metastatic disease.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill cancer cells in the body.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, often before or after surgery, and sometimes as the main treatment for metastatic bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer that uses drugs to kill cancer cells throughout the body.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,drugs,option,treatment,treats\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Papillary Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"RAI Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic papillary thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"thyroid cancer,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Care Team\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"The care team may provide chemotherapy as part of treatment.\", \"keywords\": \"provides,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Metastatic Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"RAI Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is recommended for metastatic papillary, follicular, or oncocytic thyroid cancers that take up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"metastatic,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, often given in combination or with stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method for primary CNS lymphoma, often using drugs like methotrexate.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill lymphoma cells, and high-dose chemotherapy is part of stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treated with,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 5.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Oncocytic Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"RAI Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic oncocytic thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"thyroid cancer,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy may be combined with chemotherapy as an alternative treatment option when surgical margins are inadequate.<SEP>Radiation therapy is used in addition to chemotherapy for metastatic anal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"combination,combined with,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Follicular Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"RAI Therapy\", \"description\": \"RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic follicular thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"thyroid cancer,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy is part of chemoradiation for anal cancer and can cause infertility.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for anal cancer, often combined with chemotherapy.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Thyroid Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Total Thyroidectomy\", \"description\": \"Total thyroidectomy is a treatment for thyroid cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"treats\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"AML\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is the standard of care for treating AML.\", \"keywords\": \"standard of care,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Targeted Therapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Thyroid Cancer\", \"description\": \"Targeted therapy is used for certain thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI therapy, have returned, or have metastasized.<SEP>Targeted therapy may be an option for thyroid cancers that don't respond to other treatments.<SEP>Targeted therapy may be an option for thyroid cancer that doesn't take up iodine.\", \"keywords\": \"targeted,treated with,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Immunotherapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Immunotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"non-hormone,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Radioactive Iodine Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A treatment using radioactive iodine to kill thyroid cancer cells left in the body after surgery.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "RAI Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Radioactive iodine therapy, a treatment for thyroid cancer, mentioned as a standard treatment that targeted therapy is used when RAI therapy cannot be performed.<SEP>Radioactive iodine therapy used to destroy remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells after surgery.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Total Thyroidectomy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A surgical procedure that removes the entire thyroid gland, often with nearby lymph nodes if cancer is suspected.<SEP>Surgical removal of the entire thyroid gland.<SEP>Surgical removal of the entire thyroid, recommended for suspected follicular or oncocytic tumors that have grown beyond the thyroid or are larger than 4 cm.<SEP>A surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, often used to treat thyroid cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Medullary Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The third most common type of thyroid cancer, which can be inherited and is the focus of a specific chapter.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that does not take up iodine and is not effectively treated with RAI therapy.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that may have RET gene fusion.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Differentiated Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of thyroid cancer that takes up iodine and is often treated with RAI therapy after thyroidectomy.<SEP>A group of thyroid cancers that arise from follicular cells and retain some normal thyroid tissue features.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A treatment using iodine-131 to selectively kill thyroid cancer cells, administered orally, often after thyroidectomy for high-risk cancers.<SEP>A treatment that uses radioactive iodine to destroy remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Papillary Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "disease", "description": "The most common type of thyroid cancer, often slow-growing.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of cancer that forms in the thyroid gland, with several types, most curable with appropriate treatment.<SEP>Cancer that forms in the thyroid gland, with several types including papillary, follicular, oncocytic, medullary, and anaplastic.<SEP>A disease characterized by malignant cells in the thyroid gland, with different types including differentiated, medullary, and anaplastic.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the thyroid gland. Targeted therapies are used for certain thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI therapy, have returned, or have metastasized.<SEP>A cancer that forms in the tissues of the thyroid gland.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects the thyroid gland, with metastatic forms that may require targeted therapy or monitoring.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Oncocytic Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Iodine-131", "entity_type": "naturalobject", "description": "A radioactive isotope of iodine used in RAI therapy and whole-body scans.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Thyroidectomy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Surgical removal of the thyroid gland, often performed before RAI therapy for thyroid cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Whole-Body RAI Imaging", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Imaging test to look for remaining thyroid cancer cells after RAI therapy.<SEP>Imaging after radioactive iodine therapy to detect remaining cancer cells.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Follicular Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of thyroid cancer that can be caused by Cowden syndrome.<SEP>A type of thyroid cancer that can be treated with RAI therapy if it takes up iodine.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Metastatic Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Metastatic Cancer is a term used to describe cancer that has spread from its original (primary) site to other, distant parts of the body. This process of spread can involve various organs and tissues, with common distant sites including the lungs, liver, bones, and brain, as well as distant lymph nodes. The condition is often characterized as very advanced disease, and when cancer is identified as stage 4 at the time of diagnosis, it is considered metastatic from the outset. In specific contexts, such as esophageal cancer, the metastatic stage specifically denotes that the disease has spread to distant bodily locations. Regardless of the primary tumor's origin, the defining feature of metastatic cancer is its presence in areas far removed from the initial site of development.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682934}, {"entity_name": "Whole-Body Radioiodine Scan", "entity_type": "method", "description": "An imaging procedure performed after RAI therapy to detect remaining thyroid tissue or hidden cancer areas.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Metastatic Thyroid Cancer", "entity_type": "UNKNOWN", "description": "Joining a clinical trial is strongly encouraged for everyone with metastatic thyroid cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"entity_name": "Targeted Therapy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Targeted Therapy is a form of systemic cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to identify and attack specific molecules, genes, proteins, or receptors involved in the growth, spread, and survival of cancer cells. Unlike broad-spectrum treatments like chemotherapy, this approach focuses on particular genetic changes, mutations (e.g., PIK3CA), or biomarkers (e.g., PARP inhibitors) present in tumors, making it effective for cancers with certain molecular features. By targeting these specific parts of cancer cells, the therapy aims to slow or stop tumor progression, and it may potentially cause fewer side effects than chemotherapy, though it can also be combined with chemotherapy in certain contexts, such as for primary CNS lymphoma.\n\nThis therapy is applicable across various cancer types, including advanced-stage prostate cancer, recurrent and/or metastatic cervical cancer, and thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy, have recurred, or have metastasized and continue to grow. For thyroid cancer, the targeted therapies currently used are kinase inhibitors, typically administered as swallowed capsules. However, the treatment is not without risks; side effects can include body aches, rash, high blood pressure, and abnormal bleeding, and some agents may adversely affect the heart, skin, and digestive system. Additionally, targeted therapy can impact fertility, and its use is often guided by biomarker testing to determine patient eligibility.\n\nAs a systemic therapy, it is generally reserved for cases where specific genetic targets are identified, and it may be employed when other treatments have failed or are unsuitable. The overarching goal remains to attack specific types of cancer cells while minimizing harm to normal cells, thereby offering a more precise treatment option for individuals with particular gene mutations or molecular profiles.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682881}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Advanced prostate cancer can be treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "non-hormone,treated by,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Radioactive Iodine Therapy", "tgt_id": "Total Thyroidectomy", "description": "RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy to kill any remaining thyroid cancer cells.", "keywords": "adjuvant treatment,residual cancer", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-151", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Chemotherapy may be needed before surgery if ovarian cancer is advanced.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat ovarian cancer, sometimes before surgery.<SEP>Chemotherapy may be given first to shrink ovarian cancer if surgery is not initially an option.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method used for ovarian cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "neoadjuvant,shrink tumor,systemic therapy,treatment", "weight": 6.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-046<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Differentiated Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "description": "Differentiated thyroid cancers that take up iodine are often treated with RAI therapy after total thyroidectomy.", "keywords": "responds,treated with", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used to treat cervical cancer, often in combination with radiation.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "tgt_id": "Thyroid Cancer", "description": "RAI therapy is used to treat thyroid cancer, especially differentiated types that take up iodine, to lower recurrence risk or treat spread.<SEP>RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy to kill remaining cancer cells.", "keywords": "targets,treated with,treats", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Prostate cancer can be treated with radiation therapy.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment option for prostate cancer, often used for higher-risk cases.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "management,non-hormone,treated by,treatment", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Medullary Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is not effective against medullary thyroid cancer because it does not take up iodine.", "keywords": "not effective,resistant", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer and as part of chemoradiation.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation therapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treats", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "tgt_id": "Total Thyroidectomy", "description": "RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy.", "keywords": "may be followed by", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is a treatment for cervical cancer using high-energy x-rays.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for cervical cancer.", "keywords": "radiation,treatment,treatment for", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Iodine-131", "tgt_id": "Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy uses iodine-131 to selectively kill thyroid cancer cells that take up iodine.", "keywords": "radioactive isotope,uses", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, either directly into the bladder or systemically for metastatic disease.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill cancer cells in the body.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, often before or after surgery, and sometimes as the main treatment for metastatic bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer that uses drugs to kill cancer cells throughout the body.", "keywords": "cancer,drugs,option,treatment,treats", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Papillary Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "RAI Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic papillary thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.", "keywords": "thyroid cancer,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Care Team", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "The care team may provide chemotherapy as part of treatment.", "keywords": "provides,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Cancer", "tgt_id": "RAI Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is recommended for metastatic papillary, follicular, or oncocytic thyroid cancers that take up iodine.", "keywords": "metastatic,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "Primary CNS lymphoma is treated with chemotherapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, often given in combination or with stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment method for primary CNS lymphoma, often using drugs like methotrexate.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill lymphoma cells, and high-dose chemotherapy is part of stem cell rescue.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "cancer,treated with,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Oncocytic Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "RAI Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic oncocytic thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.", "keywords": "thyroid cancer,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Chemotherapy", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy may be combined with chemotherapy as an alternative treatment option when surgical margins are inadequate.<SEP>Radiation therapy is used in addition to chemotherapy for metastatic anal cancer.", "keywords": "combination,combined with,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Follicular Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "RAI Therapy", "description": "RAI therapy is recommended to treat metastatic follicular thyroid cancer that takes up iodine.", "keywords": "thyroid cancer,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is part of chemoradiation for anal cancer and can cause infertility.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for anal cancer, often combined with chemotherapy.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Thyroid Cancer", "tgt_id": "Total Thyroidectomy", "description": "Total thyroidectomy is a treatment for thyroid cancer.", "keywords": "treats", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "AML", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is the standard of care for treating AML.", "keywords": "standard of care,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-039", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Targeted Therapy", "tgt_id": "Thyroid Cancer", "description": "Targeted therapy is used for certain thyroid cancers that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI therapy, have returned, or have metastasized.<SEP>Targeted therapy may be an option for thyroid cancers that don't respond to other treatments.<SEP>Targeted therapy may be an option for thyroid cancer that doesn't take up iodine.", "keywords": "targeted,treated with,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Immunotherapy", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Immunotherapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a non-hormone therapy option for advanced prostate cancer.", "keywords": "non-hormone,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5812, "output_tokens": 115, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1918.62500004936, "generation_time_ms": 1240.1527999900281, "total_time_ms": 3159.5161000732332, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["radioactive iodine therapy", "medullary thyroid cancer", "total thyroidectomy", "alternative therapies"], "low_level": ["medullary thyroid cancer", "total thyroidectomy", "radioactive iodine therapy"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 53, "total_relations_found": 325, "entities_after_truncation": 17, "relations_after_truncation": 25, "merged_chunks_count": 57, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "Why is radioactive iodine therapy not used for medullary thyroid cancer after total thyroidectomy, and what alternative therapies are recommended?", "question_type": "Complex Reasoning", "ground_truth": "RAI therapy is ineffective for medullary thyroid cancer, so targeted therapy and thyroid hormone replacement are recommended alternatives.", "evidence": "Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is ineffective for medullary thyroid cancer.; Targeted therapy is recommended for medullary thyroid cancer.; Thyroid hormone replacement is recommended for medullary thyroid cancer.", "evidence_relations": "RAI therapy is not effective for medullary or anaplastic thyroid cancer; Medullary thyroid cancer Treatment: Total thyroidectomy, Neck dissection, Targeted therapy (if advanced/metastatic), Thyroid hormone replacement therapy", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-6e3e48fa", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the context, essential diagnostic procedures for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma include a **biopsy** and **immunophenotyping**. The context also mentions that testing for CBCL includes general health tests, skin exam, blood tests, imaging tests, biomarker and genetic changes, and TNM scores.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Papules\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Small pimples that cutaneous B-cell lymphoma nodules may look like.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of lymphoma that starts in B cells and appears on the skin.<SEP>A type of primary cutaneous lymphoma that develops from abnormal B cells, often appearing as nodules under the skin that may look like small pimples called papules.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects B-cells in the skin, requiring accurate testing for diagnosis and treatment.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Skin Lesions\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Abnormal growths or sores on the skin that may indicate basal cell skin cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682777}, {\"entity_name\": \"Physical Exam\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Physical Exam is a broad clinical procedure performed by healthcare providers to assess a patient's overall health status, detect signs of disease, and inform diagnosis and treatment decisions. It is a fundamental component of medical care, utilized in various contexts such as the diagnosis of basal cell skin cancer, testing for DCIS, determining the clinical stage before treatment, and serving as a surveillance method for stages 2 and 3 colon cancer. During oncology care, it is performed by an oncologist every 1 to 3 cycles of chemotherapy to monitor the patient's condition and is also used during surveillance to detect potential recurrence.\\n\\nThe examination typically involves checking vital signs, measuring height and weight, listening to the lungs and heart, and examining the eyes, ears, nose, and throat. Providers also palpate body parts to assess organs and feel for enlarged lymph nodes, particularly in the neck, underarms, and groin. Depending on the clinical context, the exam may include specific assessments, such as a breast examination to check for abnormalities or palpation of the abdomen and pelvis to detect masses or other issues. It may also involve evaluating body systems and functional status to gauge physical ability.\\n\\nAs a general health check, the physical exam is a routine method for checking the body for signs of disease and assessing overall physical condition. It is a versatile tool used by various team members to evaluate a patient's health, detect abnormalities, and guide subsequent medical actions.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682794}, {\"entity_name\": \"Nodules\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Small, round areas of tissue in the liver that may be detected by ultrasound; not all are cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Testing for CBCL\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The process of testing for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma, including general health tests, skin exam, blood tests, imaging tests, biopsy, immunophenotyping, biomarker and genetic changes, and TNM scores.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682854}, {\"entity_name\": \"Medical History\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Medical History is a comprehensive record of a patient's past and current health information, encompassing all health issues, illnesses, injuries, symptoms, treatments, and medications a person has experienced throughout their life. This record serves as a foundational tool for healthcare providers, informing the care team about the patient's overall health status and guiding clinical decision-making. It is actively used to determine the best course of treatment for a patient, including in specific diagnostic contexts such as the evaluation of basal cell skin cancer. The information contained within a medical history is typically gathered during a medical consultation and may also include the health history of close blood relatives, providing a broader context for the patient's health. Ultimately, this detailed review of a patient's past and present health conditions is essential for informing medical decisions and ensuring appropriate, personalized care.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682796}, {\"entity_name\": \"Nonmelanoma skin cancers\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A group of skin cancers that includes basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers, also called keratinocyte carcinomas.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682837}, {\"entity_name\": \"Biopsy\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Biopsy is a medical procedure that involves the removal of a sample of tissue, cells, or fluid from the body for examination, typically by a pathologist, to diagnose cancer and determine tumor histology. It is used to confirm the presence of cancer, identify its type, and guide treatment decisions. The procedure is essential for diagnosing various cancers, including basal cell skin cancer, adrenal tumors, follicular lymphoma, ovarian cancer, pancreatic cancer, prostate cancer, DCIS, primary CNS lymphoma, thyroid cancer, bone cancer, brain tumors, bladder cancer, and anal cancer. Biopsies can be performed using different techniques, such as fine-needle aspiration, core needle biopsy, or surgical removal, and may be guided by imaging methods like ultrasound or CT. In some cases, a biopsy is performed during initial surgery, such as for ovarian cancer, or before chemotherapy if recommended. For prostate cancer, a biopsy is often decided after PSA test and digital rectal exam results, and the procedure can temporarily raise PSA levels. A biopsy may also be necessary before starting steroid therapy, as in the case of primary CNS lymphoma. The procedure is the only way to definitively confirm cancer in many cases, and it can also be used for staging, such as in clinical staging, or to confirm cancer progression. Specific types include bone marrow aspirate and biopsy for follicular lymphoma, endometrial biopsy, and Pap test. A biopsy is a key diagnostic tool that removes a small piece of tissue or tumor for laboratory testing, ensuring an accurate diagnosis and informing subsequent treatment plans.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-022<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682783}, {\"entity_name\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in lymphocytes within the central nervous system (CNS). It most commonly affects the brain, often developing deep within brain tissue, but can also occur in the spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and the eyes. The disease can spread within the nervous system but rarely metastasizes to other areas of the body. Diagnosis typically requires a biopsy and imaging tests to confirm the presence of the malignancy.\\n\\nAs a specific type of lymphoma confined to the CNS, it is the subject of various therapeutic approaches discussed in medical literature. Treatment options for this condition are actively explored, with multiple therapies being considered or applied to manage the disease. The cancer's aggressive nature and its location within the CNS make it a challenging condition to treat, necessitating specialized medical intervention.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683084}, {\"entity_name\": \"Skin Exams\", \"entity_type\": \"method\", \"description\": \"Regular check-ups of the skin and lymph nodes to monitor for recurrence of skin cancer.<SEP>Regular examinations of the skin by a doctor to check for signs of skin cancer recurrence or new skin cancers.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682901}, {\"entity_name\": \"Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A subtype of AML that may require additional biopsies.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683036}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer may spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Prostate cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"may spread to,metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Physical Exam\", \"description\": \"Physical exam is part of the tests for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis,examination\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Lymph nodes may be removed during ovarian cancer surgery to check for cancer spread.\", \"keywords\": \"removal,surgery\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Testing for CBCL\", \"description\": \"Testing for CBCL is needed to diagnose and treat cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"testing for\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Anal cancer cells can travel through lymphatics or blood to lymph nodes, including inguinal lymph nodes.<SEP>Anal cancer staging assesses whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes (N stage).\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread,staging\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Medical History\", \"description\": \"Medical history helps determine the best treatment for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis,treatment planning\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy is a treatment option for people who cannot or choose not to have surgery, and may be used after surgery to kill remaining cancer cells.<SEP>Radiation therapy is often given after surgery to kill leftover cancer cells and reduce recurrence risk.\", \"keywords\": \"post-surgery,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary CNS Lymphoma\", \"description\": \"A biopsy is key to confirming the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>A biopsy is the only way to confirm a diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"confirmation,diagnosed by,diagnosis\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Biopsy is used to sample lymph nodes that appear swollen or abnormal to check for cancer spread.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnostic procedure\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)\", \"tgt_id\": \"Skin Lesion Biopsy\", \"description\": \"If BPDCN is suspected, a skin lesion biopsy may be performed.\", \"keywords\": \"may require\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy aims radiation beams at lymph nodes in the pelvis and groin.\", \"keywords\": \"targeting\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Skin Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"A skin biopsy is needed to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>A skin biopsy is used to remove a sample and test it for cancer, aiding in the diagnosis of squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>A skin biopsy is used to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer by removing a tissue sample for analysis.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis,diagnostic method,diagnostic procedure,procedure\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683096}, {\"src_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer is the second most common cancer in American males, after skin cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"comparison\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas\", \"description\": \"Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma is a type of primary cutaneous lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"type of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"Cancer cells in lymph nodes indicate a higher risk of spread in squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>Lymph nodes are common sites for squamous cell skin cancer to spread, and their involvement determines staging.\", \"keywords\": \"risk,spread,staging\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"NCCN Guidelines for Patients\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, Leg Type\", \"description\": \"Treatment for PC-DLBCL, leg type can be found in NCCN Guidelines for Patients.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment found in\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Basal Cell Skin Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"description\": \"Biopsy is used to diagnose basal cell skin cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis,testing\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, Leg Type\", \"description\": \"Primary cutaneous diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type is a type of cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.\", \"keywords\": \"type of\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Imaging Tests\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Imaging tests can show leukemia spread to lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"spread detection\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Node Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"A lymph node biopsy is done if spread to lymph nodes is suspected in squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>Lymph node biopsy is performed if lymph nodes near a squamous cell skin cancer are enlarged.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnostic,diagnostic procedure,lymph nodes\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-008\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Basal Cell Skin Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy may be used to treat basal cell skin cancer in some cases.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683092}, {\"src_id\": \"Regional CSCC\", \"tgt_id\": \"Surgery\", \"description\": \"Surgery is recommended to treat squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"recommended,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Surgery\", \"description\": \"Surgery is used to remove tumors and nearby lymph nodes in squamous cell skin cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"removal,treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Blood Test\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia\", \"description\": \"CLL is confirmed with a blood test.\", \"keywords\": \"confirms,diagnostic\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Throat Cancer\", \"description\": \"Cancer cells often spread to lymph nodes in the neck.<SEP>Throat tumors often spread to nearby lymph nodes in the head and neck.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread,spreads to\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Acute Myeloid Leukemia\", \"tgt_id\": \"Bone Marrow Biopsy\", \"description\": \"A bone marrow biopsy is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.<SEP>AML diagnosis is confirmed using bone marrow biopsy.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosed with,diagnosis,procedure\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683101}, {\"src_id\": \"Acute Myeloid Leukemia\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Acute Myeloid Leukemia can cause enlarged lymph nodes, as indicated by the instruction to feel for enlarged lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, and groin.\", \"keywords\": \"can cause enlargement in\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma Of The Vermilion Lip\", \"tgt_id\": \"NCCN Guidelines For Patients: Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"description\": \"Treatment information for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma of the vermilion lip is found in these guidelines.\", \"keywords\": \"guidelines,treatment information\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Squamous Cell Skin Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Systemic Therapy\", \"description\": \"Systemic therapy is not used often for CSCC, except when it has spread elsewhere in the body.<SEP>Systemic therapy is used when cancer has spread or cannot be removed by surgery or radiation.\", \"keywords\": \"advanced disease,treatment,treatment option\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Papules", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Small pimples that cutaneous B-cell lymphoma nodules may look like.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of lymphoma that starts in B cells and appears on the skin.<SEP>A type of primary cutaneous lymphoma that develops from abnormal B cells, often appearing as nodules under the skin that may look like small pimples called papules.<SEP>A type of cancer that affects B-cells in the skin, requiring accurate testing for diagnosis and treatment.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Skin Lesions", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Abnormal growths or sores on the skin that may indicate basal cell skin cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682777}, {"entity_name": "Physical Exam", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Physical Exam is a broad clinical procedure performed by healthcare providers to assess a patient's overall health status, detect signs of disease, and inform diagnosis and treatment decisions. It is a fundamental component of medical care, utilized in various contexts such as the diagnosis of basal cell skin cancer, testing for DCIS, determining the clinical stage before treatment, and serving as a surveillance method for stages 2 and 3 colon cancer. During oncology care, it is performed by an oncologist every 1 to 3 cycles of chemotherapy to monitor the patient's condition and is also used during surveillance to detect potential recurrence.\n\nThe examination typically involves checking vital signs, measuring height and weight, listening to the lungs and heart, and examining the eyes, ears, nose, and throat. Providers also palpate body parts to assess organs and feel for enlarged lymph nodes, particularly in the neck, underarms, and groin. Depending on the clinical context, the exam may include specific assessments, such as a breast examination to check for abnormalities or palpation of the abdomen and pelvis to detect masses or other issues. It may also involve evaluating body systems and functional status to gauge physical ability.\n\nAs a general health check, the physical exam is a routine method for checking the body for signs of disease and assessing overall physical condition. It is a versatile tool used by various team members to evaluate a patient's health, detect abnormalities, and guide subsequent medical actions.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682794}, {"entity_name": "Nodules", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Small, round areas of tissue in the liver that may be detected by ultrasound; not all are cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Testing for CBCL", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The process of testing for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma, including general health tests, skin exam, blood tests, imaging tests, biopsy, immunophenotyping, biomarker and genetic changes, and TNM scores.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682854}, {"entity_name": "Medical History", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Medical History is a comprehensive record of a patient's past and current health information, encompassing all health issues, illnesses, injuries, symptoms, treatments, and medications a person has experienced throughout their life. This record serves as a foundational tool for healthcare providers, informing the care team about the patient's overall health status and guiding clinical decision-making. It is actively used to determine the best course of treatment for a patient, including in specific diagnostic contexts such as the evaluation of basal cell skin cancer. The information contained within a medical history is typically gathered during a medical consultation and may also include the health history of close blood relatives, providing a broader context for the patient's health. Ultimately, this detailed review of a patient's past and present health conditions is essential for informing medical decisions and ensuring appropriate, personalized care.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682796}, {"entity_name": "Nonmelanoma skin cancers", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A group of skin cancers that includes basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers, also called keratinocyte carcinomas.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682837}, {"entity_name": "Biopsy", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Biopsy is a medical procedure that involves the removal of a sample of tissue, cells, or fluid from the body for examination, typically by a pathologist, to diagnose cancer and determine tumor histology. It is used to confirm the presence of cancer, identify its type, and guide treatment decisions. The procedure is essential for diagnosing various cancers, including basal cell skin cancer, adrenal tumors, follicular lymphoma, ovarian cancer, pancreatic cancer, prostate cancer, DCIS, primary CNS lymphoma, thyroid cancer, bone cancer, brain tumors, bladder cancer, and anal cancer. Biopsies can be performed using different techniques, such as fine-needle aspiration, core needle biopsy, or surgical removal, and may be guided by imaging methods like ultrasound or CT. In some cases, a biopsy is performed during initial surgery, such as for ovarian cancer, or before chemotherapy if recommended. For prostate cancer, a biopsy is often decided after PSA test and digital rectal exam results, and the procedure can temporarily raise PSA levels. A biopsy may also be necessary before starting steroid therapy, as in the case of primary CNS lymphoma. The procedure is the only way to definitively confirm cancer in many cases, and it can also be used for staging, such as in clinical staging, or to confirm cancer progression. Specific types include bone marrow aspirate and biopsy for follicular lymphoma, endometrial biopsy, and Pap test. A biopsy is a key diagnostic tool that removes a small piece of tissue or tumor for laboratory testing, ensuring an accurate diagnosis and informing subsequent treatment plans.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-022<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682783}, {"entity_name": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in lymphocytes within the central nervous system (CNS). It most commonly affects the brain, often developing deep within brain tissue, but can also occur in the spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and the eyes. The disease can spread within the nervous system but rarely metastasizes to other areas of the body. Diagnosis typically requires a biopsy and imaging tests to confirm the presence of the malignancy.\n\nAs a specific type of lymphoma confined to the CNS, it is the subject of various therapeutic approaches discussed in medical literature. Treatment options for this condition are actively explored, with multiple therapies being considered or applied to manage the disease. The cancer's aggressive nature and its location within the CNS make it a challenging condition to treat, necessitating specialized medical intervention.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683084}, {"entity_name": "Skin Exams", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Regular check-ups of the skin and lymph nodes to monitor for recurrence of skin cancer.<SEP>Regular examinations of the skin by a doctor to check for signs of skin cancer recurrence or new skin cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682901}, {"entity_name": "Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A subtype of AML that may require additional biopsies.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683036}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer may spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Prostate cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.", "keywords": "may spread to,metastasis,spread", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Physical Exam", "description": "Physical exam is part of the tests for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.", "keywords": "diagnosis,examination", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Lymph nodes may be removed during ovarian cancer surgery to check for cancer spread.", "keywords": "removal,surgery", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Testing for CBCL", "description": "Testing for CBCL is needed to diagnose and treat cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.", "keywords": "testing for", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Anal cancer cells can travel through lymphatics or blood to lymph nodes, including inguinal lymph nodes.<SEP>Anal cancer staging assesses whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes (N stage).", "keywords": "metastasis,spread,staging", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Medical History", "description": "Medical history helps determine the best treatment for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.", "keywords": "diagnosis,treatment planning", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Radiation Therapy", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "Radiation therapy is a treatment option for people who cannot or choose not to have surgery, and may be used after surgery to kill remaining cancer cells.<SEP>Radiation therapy is often given after surgery to kill leftover cancer cells and reduce recurrence risk.", "keywords": "post-surgery,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Primary CNS Lymphoma", "description": "A biopsy is key to confirming the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.<SEP>A biopsy is the only way to confirm a diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.", "keywords": "confirmation,diagnosed by,diagnosis", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Biopsy is used to sample lymph nodes that appear swollen or abnormal to check for cancer spread.", "keywords": "diagnostic procedure", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)", "tgt_id": "Skin Lesion Biopsy", "description": "If BPDCN is suspected, a skin lesion biopsy may be performed.", "keywords": "may require", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy aims radiation beams at lymph nodes in the pelvis and groin.", "keywords": "targeting", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Skin Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "A skin biopsy is needed to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>A skin biopsy is used to remove a sample and test it for cancer, aiding in the diagnosis of squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>A skin biopsy is used to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer by removing a tissue sample for analysis.", "keywords": "diagnosis,diagnostic method,diagnostic procedure,procedure", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683096}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Skin Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer is the second most common cancer in American males, after skin cancer.", "keywords": "comparison", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas", "description": "Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma is a type of primary cutaneous lymphoma.", "keywords": "type of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "Cancer cells in lymph nodes indicate a higher risk of spread in squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>Lymph nodes are common sites for squamous cell skin cancer to spread, and their involvement determines staging.", "keywords": "risk,spread,staging", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "NCCN Guidelines for Patients", "tgt_id": "Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, Leg Type", "description": "Treatment for PC-DLBCL, leg type can be found in NCCN Guidelines for Patients.", "keywords": "treatment found in", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Basal Cell Skin Cancer", "tgt_id": "Biopsy", "description": "Biopsy is used to diagnose basal cell skin cancer.", "keywords": "diagnosis,testing", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma", "tgt_id": "Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, Leg Type", "description": "Primary cutaneous diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type is a type of cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.", "keywords": "type of", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Imaging Tests", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Imaging tests can show leukemia spread to lymph nodes.", "keywords": "spread detection", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Lymph Node Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "A lymph node biopsy is done if spread to lymph nodes is suspected in squamous cell skin cancer.<SEP>Lymph node biopsy is performed if lymph nodes near a squamous cell skin cancer are enlarged.", "keywords": "diagnostic,diagnostic procedure,lymph nodes", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-008", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Basal Cell Skin Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy may be used to treat basal cell skin cancer in some cases.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"src_id": "Regional CSCC", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is recommended to treat squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.", "keywords": "recommended,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is used to remove tumors and nearby lymph nodes in squamous cell skin cancer.", "keywords": "removal,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Blood Test", "tgt_id": "Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia", "description": "CLL is confirmed with a blood test.", "keywords": "confirms,diagnostic", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Throat Cancer", "description": "Cancer cells often spread to lymph nodes in the neck.<SEP>Throat tumors often spread to nearby lymph nodes in the head and neck.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread,spreads to", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "tgt_id": "Bone Marrow Biopsy", "description": "A bone marrow biopsy is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.<SEP>AML diagnosis is confirmed using bone marrow biopsy.", "keywords": "diagnosed with,diagnosis,procedure", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia can cause enlarged lymph nodes, as indicated by the instruction to feel for enlarged lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, and groin.", "keywords": "can cause enlargement in", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma Of The Vermilion Lip", "tgt_id": "NCCN Guidelines For Patients: Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "description": "Treatment information for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma of the vermilion lip is found in these guidelines.", "keywords": "guidelines,treatment information", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Squamous Cell Skin Cancer", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Systemic therapy is not used often for CSCC, except when it has spread elsewhere in the body.<SEP>Systemic therapy is used when cancer has spread or cannot be removed by surgery or radiation.", "keywords": "advanced disease,treatment,treatment option", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5843, "output_tokens": 96, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 1684.0421000961214, "generation_time_ms": 1191.9204000150785, "total_time_ms": 2876.614399952814, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["diagnostic procedures", "confirming a case", "cutaneous B-cell lymphoma"], "low_level": ["skin nodules"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 81, "total_relations_found": 273, "entities_after_truncation": 13, "relations_after_truncation": 29, "merged_chunks_count": 52, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "Which diagnostic procedures are essential for confirming a case of cutaneous B-cell lymphoma presenting with skin nodules?", "question_type": "Complex Reasoning", "ground_truth": "Skin exam, blood tests, imaging tests, biopsy, and immunophenotyping are essential for confirming CBCL with nodules.", "evidence": "Skin exam is essential for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma with skin nodules.; Blood tests are essential for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma with skin nodules.; Imaging tests are essential for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma with skin nodules.; Biopsy is essential for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma with skin nodules.; Immunophenotyping is essential for confirming cutaneous B-cell lymphoma with skin nodules.", "evidence_relations": "Diagnosis of CBCL involves skin exam, blood tests, imaging tests, biopsy, and immunophenotyping; Symptoms of CBCL include skin nodules and papules", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-7fba506d", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the provided context, the anatomical location within the stomach influences the potential spread of cancer to nearby organs through the lymphatic system. The context notes that lymph nodes are located \"along the stomach's curvatures\" and are common sites for stomach cancer metastasis. However, the context does not provide specific details on how the location within the stomach (e.g., cardia, fundus, body, antrum, pylorus) affects the spread to specific nearby organs.", "contexts": ["Entities:\n[{\"entity_name\": \"Peritoneum\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"The abdominal lining, a common site for metastasis in stomach cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Anatomic Staging\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Staging based on extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).<SEP>Staging based on the extent of cancer as defined by TNM.<SEP>A staging method based on the extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).<SEP>Anatomic staging is based on the extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683031}, {\"entity_name\": \"Metastatic Stomach Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A stage of stomach cancer where the cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, such as the liver, peritoneum, or distant lymph nodes.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683087}, {\"entity_name\": \"Cancer Staging\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A way to describe the extent of cancer at the time of diagnosis, used to reflect prognosis and guide treatment decisions.<SEP>Cancer staging is a process to describe the extent of cancer in the body, including tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, used to guide treatment and predict prognosis.<SEP>The process of determining the size, location, and spread of cancer.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683079}, {\"entity_name\": \"Metastasis\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"Metastasis is the biological process by which cancer spreads from its primary, or original, site to other parts of the body. This dissemination occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or lymphatic system, enabling them to establish new tumors in distant or nearby anatomical locations. The process is a defining characteristic of advanced-stage cancer, as it marks the transition from a localized disease to a systemic one.\\n\\nThe specific destinations for metastatic spread vary depending on the origin of the primary cancer. Common sites of secondary tumor formation include the lymph nodes, lungs, bones, and liver. For instance, metastasis from prostate cancer defines advanced-stage disease, while bladder cancer may spread to lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs. In the context of kidney cancer, metastasis refers to the formation of new tumors outside the kidney itself.\\n\\nClinically, the presence of metastasis often necessitates further diagnostic procedures, such as a biopsy, to confirm the spread and guide treatment decisions. The formation of these secondary tumors is a critical factor in cancer prognosis, as it indicates that the disease has become more complex and challenging to treat.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682892}, {\"entity_name\": \"Liver\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"The liver is a large organ located in the abdomen, on the right side of the body under the rib cage, and is a central component of the digestive system. It performs numerous vital functions, including filtering blood to remove toxins, producing bile to break down fats, processing nutrients and sugar for energy, synthesizing coagulation factors to aid in blood clotting, and producing lymph. Its health and function are commonly assessed through blood tests, such as a chemistry profile or liver function tests, and it can be imaged using MRI to detect signs of disease.\\n\\nThe liver is a frequent and clinically significant site for cancer metastasis. It is a common destination for cancer cells originating from various primary tumors, including breast, colon, rectal, anal, stomach, pancreatic, cervical, bladder, kidney, prostate, throat, and nasopharyngeal cancers. In many of these cases, such as with colon cancer, the liver is a primary site for stage 4 spread, while for others, like kidney cancer, it is a less frequent but still possible site of distant metastasis. The liver can also be affected by leukemia spreading outside the bloodstream, and cancer cell buildup in the organ can cause it to swell.\\n\\nBeyond its role in metastasis, the liver is susceptible to damage from certain medical treatments, such as high-dose methotrexate. As an organ that processes chemicals, its function is critical to overall health, and its assessment is a standard part of medical diagnostics.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682953}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stomach\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"Organ where food passes from the esophagus and is broken down.<SEP>Organ that meets the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction, containing acid.<SEP>An organ near the liver.<SEP>A muscular, hollow organ that secretes enzymes and acid to aid digestion.<SEP>A digestive organ that receives food from the esophagus, mixes it with enzymes and acid, and releases chyme into the small intestine. It has parts including cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682946}, {\"entity_name\": \"Stomach Cancer\", \"entity_type\": \"concept\", \"description\": \"A type of cancer that originates in the stomach; if it spreads to the lungs, it is still stomach cancer.<SEP>A disease that affects the stomach.<SEP>Cancer that starts in the stomach, most commonly adenocarcinomas, which begin in mucus-producing cells.<SEP>A disease characterized by the abnormal growth of cells in the stomach lining, often developing slowly over many years.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682938}, {\"entity_name\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"entity_type\": \"location\", \"description\": \"Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that are integral components of the immune and lymphatic systems, distributed throughout the body, including high concentrations in the neck, groin, armpits, and along the stomach's curvatures. They function as filters for lymph fluid, removing foreign particles, germs, and harmful cells, including cancer cells, while also producing immune cells like lymphocytes. With over 300 nodes present in the head and neck region alone, they serve as critical sites for disease surveillance and immune response.\\n\\nThese glands are common and clinically significant sites for cancer metastasis across numerous malignancies. Basal cell skin cancer, regional cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), rectal cancer, kidney cancer, prostate cancer, bladder cancer, anal cancer, stomach cancer, and thyroid cancer can all spread to nearby lymph nodes. Additionally, they are involved in systemic diseases like leukemia, where cancer can spread outside the bloodstream, and in follicular lymphoma and B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma, where tumors or malignant cell growths form within the nodes. In conditions like small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL), cancer cells may accumulate in these organs.\\n\\nLymph nodes are routinely evaluated in cancer staging and treatment planning. Enlargement of these glands in the neck, underarm, or groin is often checked during physical exams, and they may be assessed via imaging techniques such as CT scans or MRIs. In surgical procedures like colectomy, radical cystectomy, or ovarian cancer surgery, nearby lymph nodes are often removed and tested for the presence of cancer cells. They are also targets for radiation therapy, as seen in anal cancer treatment, where beams are directed at the pelvis and groin. While they are frequent sites of metastasis for many cancers, hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) does not commonly spread to these nodes.\", \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788682780}]\nRelationships:\n[{\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"description\": \"A history of cervical cancer increases the risk of anal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"risk factor\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Anatomic Staging\", \"tgt_id\": \"Cancer Staging\", \"description\": \"Anatomic staging is a component of cancer staging based on tumor size, lymph node status, and metastasis.<SEP>Cancer staging includes anatomic staging, which is based on tumor size, lymph node status, and distant metastasis.\", \"keywords\": \"includes,type of staging\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Chemotherapy\", \"description\": \"Chemotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer and as part of chemoradiation.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation therapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment,treats\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Liver\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach\", \"description\": \"The stomach is found near the liver.\", \"keywords\": \"near\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"description\": \"A biopsy is used to confirm the diagnosis of anal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach Cancer\", \"description\": \"Stomach cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.<SEP>Stomach cancer can spread to lymph nodes, particularly in locoregional and metastatic stages.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread,spreads to\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer may spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Prostate cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"may spread to,metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Bone\", \"tgt_id\": \"Metastatic Stomach Cancer\", \"description\": \"Metastatic stomach cancer may spread to the bone.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy is part of chemoradiation for anal cancer and can cause infertility.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for anal cancer, often combined with chemotherapy.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Metastatic Stomach Cancer\", \"description\": \"Metastatic stomach cancer can spread to distant lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Ovarian Cancer\", \"description\": \"Lymph nodes may be removed during ovarian cancer surgery to check for cancer spread.\", \"keywords\": \"removal,surgery\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma\", \"tgt_id\": \"Pharynx\", \"description\": \"The anatomy of the pharynx is relevant to understanding nasopharyngeal cancer spread.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy related to\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Patient\", \"description\": \"Patient is diagnosed with anal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"condition,diagnosis\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Liver\", \"tgt_id\": \"Rectal Cancer\", \"description\": \"Rectal cancer can spread to distant organs like the liver.<SEP>Rectal cancer can spread to the liver.\", \"keywords\": \"can spread to,metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Anal cancer cells can travel through lymphatics or blood to lymph nodes, including inguinal lymph nodes.<SEP>Anal cancer staging assesses whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes (N stage).\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread,staging\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Metastatic Stomach Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Peritoneum\", \"description\": \"Metastatic stomach cancer commonly spreads to the peritoneum.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Lungs\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer may spread to the lungs.\", \"keywords\": \"may spread to\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Lungs\", \"tgt_id\": \"Rectal Cancer\", \"description\": \"Rectal cancer can spread to distant organs like the lungs.<SEP>Rectal cancer can spread to the lungs.\", \"keywords\": \"can spread to,metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Liver\", \"description\": \"Anal cancer can spread to the liver, as detected by CT scans.<SEP>Anal cancer can metastasize to the liver in stage IV.<SEP>Anal cancer often spreads to the liver when it metastasizes.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,metastasizes to\", \"weight\": 3.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Throat Cancer\", \"description\": \"Cancer cells often spread to lymph nodes in the neck.<SEP>Throat tumors often spread to nearby lymph nodes in the head and neck.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread,spreads to\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Biopsy\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"description\": \"Biopsy is used to sample lymph nodes that appear swollen or abnormal to check for cancer spread.\", \"keywords\": \"diagnostic procedure\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"CLL\", \"tgt_id\": \"Diagnostic CT\", \"description\": \"A diagnostic CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis may be needed to look for big lymph nodes and assess the extent of cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"extent of cancer,lymph nodes\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Metastasis\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Advanced prostate cancer is characterized by metastasis, the spread to other body areas.<SEP>Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread outside the prostate to other areas, a process called metastasis.<SEP>Metastasis refers to the spread of prostate cancer to other parts of the body.<SEP>Prostate cancer may metastasize to lymph nodes, bones, or internal organs.\", \"keywords\": \"advanced-stage,cancer progression,characterized by,spread,spread of,spreading\", \"weight\": 4.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Colon\", \"tgt_id\": \"Colon Cancer\", \"description\": \"Colon cancer forms in the colon, part of the large intestine.<SEP>Colon cancer occurs in the colon.\", \"keywords\": \"anatomy,cancer,location\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Clinical Trial\", \"description\": \"Everyone with anal cancer is encouraged to consider enrolling in a clinical trial for treatment if available.<SEP>Clinical trials are research studies that may offer new treatment options for anal cancer patients.\", \"keywords\": \"research,treatment\", \"weight\": 2.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Staging System\", \"description\": \"The staging system considers the spread of cancer to nearby lymph nodes.\", \"keywords\": \"criteria,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683102}, {\"src_id\": \"Anal Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Immunotherapy\", \"description\": \"Immunotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"treatment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC\", \"tgt_id\": \"Metastatic Cancer\", \"description\": \"Some early and locally advanced cancers may spread to other organs and become metastatic.\", \"keywords\": \"cancer,progression\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683097}, {\"src_id\": \"Lung\", \"tgt_id\": \"Prostate Cancer\", \"description\": \"Prostate cancer may metastasize to the lung.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683103}, {\"src_id\": \"Lymph Nodes\", \"tgt_id\": \"Radiation Therapy\", \"description\": \"Radiation therapy aims radiation beams at lymph nodes in the pelvis and groin.\", \"keywords\": \"targeting\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683105}, {\"src_id\": \"Family History\", \"tgt_id\": \"Stomach Cancer\", \"description\": \"Family history of cancer can indicate an increased risk for stomach cancer.\", \"keywords\": \"genetics,risk assessment\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}, {\"src_id\": \"Cervical Cancer\", \"tgt_id\": \"Lungs\", \"description\": \"Cervical cancer may spread to the lungs in advanced stages.\", \"keywords\": \"metastasis,spread\", \"weight\": 1.0, \"source_id\": \"doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142\", \"file_path\": \"unknown_source\", \"created_at\": 1788683104}]"], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Peritoneum", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The abdominal lining, a common site for metastasis in stomach cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Anatomic Staging", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Staging based on extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).<SEP>Staging based on the extent of cancer as defined by TNM.<SEP>A staging method based on the extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).<SEP>Anatomic staging is based on the extent of cancer as defined by tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683031}, {"entity_name": "Metastatic Stomach Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A stage of stomach cancer where the cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, such as the liver, peritoneum, or distant lymph nodes.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Cancer Staging", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A way to describe the extent of cancer at the time of diagnosis, used to reflect prognosis and guide treatment decisions.<SEP>Cancer staging is a process to describe the extent of cancer in the body, including tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, used to guide treatment and predict prognosis.<SEP>The process of determining the size, location, and spread of cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683079}, {"entity_name": "Metastasis", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Metastasis is the biological process by which cancer spreads from its primary, or original, site to other parts of the body. This dissemination occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or lymphatic system, enabling them to establish new tumors in distant or nearby anatomical locations. The process is a defining characteristic of advanced-stage cancer, as it marks the transition from a localized disease to a systemic one.\n\nThe specific destinations for metastatic spread vary depending on the origin of the primary cancer. Common sites of secondary tumor formation include the lymph nodes, lungs, bones, and liver. For instance, metastasis from prostate cancer defines advanced-stage disease, while bladder cancer may spread to lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs. In the context of kidney cancer, metastasis refers to the formation of new tumors outside the kidney itself.\n\nClinically, the presence of metastasis often necessitates further diagnostic procedures, such as a biopsy, to confirm the spread and guide treatment decisions. The formation of these secondary tumors is a critical factor in cancer prognosis, as it indicates that the disease has become more complex and challenging to treat.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682892}, {"entity_name": "Liver", "entity_type": "location", "description": "The liver is a large organ located in the abdomen, on the right side of the body under the rib cage, and is a central component of the digestive system. It performs numerous vital functions, including filtering blood to remove toxins, producing bile to break down fats, processing nutrients and sugar for energy, synthesizing coagulation factors to aid in blood clotting, and producing lymph. Its health and function are commonly assessed through blood tests, such as a chemistry profile or liver function tests, and it can be imaged using MRI to detect signs of disease.\n\nThe liver is a frequent and clinically significant site for cancer metastasis. It is a common destination for cancer cells originating from various primary tumors, including breast, colon, rectal, anal, stomach, pancreatic, cervical, bladder, kidney, prostate, throat, and nasopharyngeal cancers. In many of these cases, such as with colon cancer, the liver is a primary site for stage 4 spread, while for others, like kidney cancer, it is a less frequent but still possible site of distant metastasis. The liver can also be affected by leukemia spreading outside the bloodstream, and cancer cell buildup in the organ can cause it to swell.\n\nBeyond its role in metastasis, the liver is susceptible to damage from certain medical treatments, such as high-dose methotrexate. As an organ that processes chemicals, its function is critical to overall health, and its assessment is a standard part of medical diagnostics.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682953}, {"entity_name": "Stomach", "entity_type": "location", "description": "Organ where food passes from the esophagus and is broken down.<SEP>Organ that meets the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction, containing acid.<SEP>An organ near the liver.<SEP>A muscular, hollow organ that secretes enzymes and acid to aid digestion.<SEP>A digestive organ that receives food from the esophagus, mixes it with enzymes and acid, and releases chyme into the small intestine. It has parts including cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682946}, {"entity_name": "Stomach Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of cancer that originates in the stomach; if it spreads to the lungs, it is still stomach cancer.<SEP>A disease that affects the stomach.<SEP>Cancer that starts in the stomach, most commonly adenocarcinomas, which begin in mucus-producing cells.<SEP>A disease characterized by the abnormal growth of cells in the stomach lining, often developing slowly over many years.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682938}, {"entity_name": "Lymph Nodes", "entity_type": "location", "description": "Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that are integral components of the immune and lymphatic systems, distributed throughout the body, including high concentrations in the neck, groin, armpits, and along the stomach's curvatures. They function as filters for lymph fluid, removing foreign particles, germs, and harmful cells, including cancer cells, while also producing immune cells like lymphocytes. With over 300 nodes present in the head and neck region alone, they serve as critical sites for disease surveillance and immune response.\n\nThese glands are common and clinically significant sites for cancer metastasis across numerous malignancies. Basal cell skin cancer, regional cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), rectal cancer, kidney cancer, prostate cancer, bladder cancer, anal cancer, stomach cancer, and thyroid cancer can all spread to nearby lymph nodes. Additionally, they are involved in systemic diseases like leukemia, where cancer can spread outside the bloodstream, and in follicular lymphoma and B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma, where tumors or malignant cell growths form within the nodes. In conditions like small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL), cancer cells may accumulate in these organs.\n\nLymph nodes are routinely evaluated in cancer staging and treatment planning. Enlargement of these glands in the neck, underarm, or groin is often checked during physical exams, and they may be assessed via imaging techniques such as CT scans or MRIs. In surgical procedures like colectomy, radical cystectomy, or ovarian cancer surgery, nearby lymph nodes are often removed and tested for the presence of cancer cells. They are also targets for radiation therapy, as seen in anal cancer treatment, where beams are directed at the pelvis and groin. While they are frequent sites of metastasis for many cancers, hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) does not commonly spread to these nodes.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682780}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Cervical Cancer", "description": "A history of cervical cancer increases the risk of anal cancer.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Anatomic Staging", "tgt_id": "Cancer Staging", "description": "Anatomic staging is a component of cancer staging based on tumor size, lymph node status, and metastasis.<SEP>Cancer staging includes anatomic staging, which is based on tumor size, lymph node status, and distant metastasis.", "keywords": "includes,type of staging", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer and as part of chemoradiation.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation therapy.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat anal cancer, often in combination with radiation.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treats", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Liver", "tgt_id": "Stomach", "description": "The stomach is found near the liver.", "keywords": "near", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Biopsy", "description": "A biopsy is used to confirm the diagnosis of anal cancer.", "keywords": "diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Stomach Cancer", "description": "Stomach cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.<SEP>Stomach cancer can spread to lymph nodes, particularly in locoregional and metastatic stages.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread,spreads to", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer may spread to lymph nodes.<SEP>Prostate cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes.", "keywords": "may spread to,metastasis,spread", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Bone", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Stomach Cancer", "description": "Metastatic stomach cancer may spread to the bone.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is part of chemoradiation for anal cancer and can cause infertility.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for anal cancer, often combined with chemotherapy.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Stomach Cancer", "description": "Metastatic stomach cancer can spread to distant lymph nodes.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Lymph nodes may be removed during ovarian cancer surgery to check for cancer spread.", "keywords": "removal,surgery", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Pharynx", "description": "The anatomy of the pharynx is relevant to understanding nasopharyngeal cancer spread.", "keywords": "anatomy related to", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Patient", "description": "Patient is diagnosed with anal cancer.", "keywords": "condition,diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Liver", "tgt_id": "Rectal Cancer", "description": "Rectal cancer can spread to distant organs like the liver.<SEP>Rectal cancer can spread to the liver.", "keywords": "can spread to,metastasis,spread", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Anal cancer cells can travel through lymphatics or blood to lymph nodes, including inguinal lymph nodes.<SEP>Anal cancer staging assesses whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes (N stage).", "keywords": "metastasis,spread,staging", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Metastatic Stomach Cancer", "tgt_id": "Peritoneum", "description": "Metastatic stomach cancer commonly spreads to the peritoneum.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Lungs", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer may spread to the lungs.", "keywords": "may spread to", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Lungs", "tgt_id": "Rectal Cancer", "description": "Rectal cancer can spread to distant organs like the lungs.<SEP>Rectal cancer can spread to the lungs.", "keywords": "can spread to,metastasis,spread", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Liver", "description": "Anal cancer can spread to the liver, as detected by CT scans.<SEP>Anal cancer can metastasize to the liver in stage IV.<SEP>Anal cancer often spreads to the liver when it metastasizes.", "keywords": "metastasis,metastasizes to", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Throat Cancer", "description": "Cancer cells often spread to lymph nodes in the neck.<SEP>Throat tumors often spread to nearby lymph nodes in the head and neck.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread,spreads to", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Lymph Nodes", "description": "Biopsy is used to sample lymph nodes that appear swollen or abnormal to check for cancer spread.", "keywords": "diagnostic procedure", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "CLL", "tgt_id": "Diagnostic CT", "description": "A diagnostic CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis may be needed to look for big lymph nodes and assess the extent of cancer.", "keywords": "extent of cancer,lymph nodes", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Metastasis", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Advanced prostate cancer is characterized by metastasis, the spread to other body areas.<SEP>Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread outside the prostate to other areas, a process called metastasis.<SEP>Metastasis refers to the spread of prostate cancer to other parts of the body.<SEP>Prostate cancer may metastasize to lymph nodes, bones, or internal organs.", "keywords": "advanced-stage,cancer progression,characterized by,spread,spread of,spreading", "weight": 4.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-089<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Colon", "tgt_id": "Colon Cancer", "description": "Colon cancer forms in the colon, part of the large intestine.<SEP>Colon cancer occurs in the colon.", "keywords": "anatomy,cancer,location", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Clinical Trial", "description": "Everyone with anal cancer is encouraged to consider enrolling in a clinical trial for treatment if available.<SEP>Clinical trials are research studies that may offer new treatment options for anal cancer patients.", "keywords": "research,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Staging System", "description": "The staging system considers the spread of cancer to nearby lymph nodes.", "keywords": "criteria,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Anal Cancer", "tgt_id": "Immunotherapy", "description": "Immunotherapy is used as primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC", "tgt_id": "Metastatic Cancer", "description": "Some early and locally advanced cancers may spread to other organs and become metastatic.", "keywords": "cancer,progression", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Lung", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Prostate cancer may metastasize to the lung.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Lymph Nodes", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy aims radiation beams at lymph nodes in the pelvis and groin.", "keywords": "targeting", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Family History", "tgt_id": "Stomach Cancer", "description": "Family history of cancer can indicate an increased risk for stomach cancer.", "keywords": "genetics,risk assessment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Cervical Cancer", "tgt_id": "Lungs", "description": "Cervical cancer may spread to the lungs in advanced stages.", "keywords": "metastasis,spread", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}], "paths": [], "input_tokens": 5809, "output_tokens": 139, "llm_calls": 2, "retrieval_time_ms": 2125.5313999718055, "generation_time_ms": 1046.2098000571132, "total_time_ms": 3172.634099959396, "retrieval_metadata": {"query_mode": "hybrid", "keywords": {"high_level": ["anatomical location", "stomach", "cancer spread", "nearby organs"], "low_level": ["stomach anatomy", "cancer metastasis"]}, "processing_info": {"total_entities_found": 76, "total_relations_found": 322, "entities_after_truncation": 9, "relations_after_truncation": 32, "merged_chunks_count": 42, "final_chunks_count": 0}}, "question": "How does the anatomical location within the stomach influence the potential spread of cancer to nearby organs?", "question_type": "Complex Reasoning", "ground_truth": "Tumors in any stomach region can invade adjacent organs such as the pancreas and spleen through local and distant spread.", "evidence": "Tumors in any stomach region can invade adjacent organs.; Adjacent organs include the pancreas.; Adjacent organs include the spleen.; Cancer can spread through local and distant mechanisms.", "evidence_relations": "Anatomical Location includes cardia, fundus, body, antrum, pylorus, and esophagogastric junction (ontology rewritten); Locoregional or locally advanced stomach cancer invades other layers of the stomach wall and/or regional lymph nodes or nearby organs; Cancer can spread to lymph nodes, veins, arteries, and organs such as the liver, pancreas, and spleen", "split": "train", "error": null}
{"question_id": "Medical-da2b7d6f", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the provided context, the information is insufficient to answer the question. The context does not specify the treatments or the surgical procedure typically used for non–muscle-invasive bladder cancer.", "contexts": ["pathologist. The pathologist will compare the cancer cells to normal cells. The more different they look, the higher the grade. And the higher the grade, the faster the cancer is expected to spread. LG means that the cancer cells are low grade (slow growing). HG means that the cancer cells are high grade (fast growing). The inner lining of the bladder is made of urothelial cells. Most cases of bladder cancer start in the urothelial cells. So this type of cancer is called urothelial cancer. But in rare cases, urothelial cell cancer has features that look like other types of cells. These are referred to as subtypes. Some of them include: Glandular subtype Squamous cell subtype Micropapillary subtype Plasmacytoid subtype Sarcomatoid subtype Nested subtype Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype Microcystic and tubular subtype It’s important to investigate whether you have one of these or another subtype. In general, treatment for these uncommon subtypes is similar in the early stages to treatment for typical urothelial cancer. However, some subtypes might grow or advance faster than typical urothelial cell cancer. And faster growth might mean it needs more aggressive treatment. Key points An important thing about bladder cancer is whether it has or it hasn’t grown into the muscle layer. Cancer that has invaded the muscle needs more aggressive treatment. The stage of cancer describes how deep it has grown into the bladder wall and how far it has spread. Cancer stage is used to determine which tests and treatments will help you receive the best outcome. Bladder cancer at stage 0 and stage 1 haven’t invaded the muscle layer. Stage 0 means that abnormal cells have grown on the inside lining of the bladder. Stage 1 means that a tumor has formed and has invaded the layer of connective tissue in the bladder wall. Stages 2, 3, and 4 bladder cancers are muscle-invasive. The tumor has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall. In stage 3 bladder cancer, the tumor has grown into the fat layer around the bladder. There may also be cancer in nearby lymph nodes. Stage 4 is metastatic bladder cancer. This is cancer that has spread outside the bladder and to distant sites in the body. Cancer grade is an estimate of how fast the cancer is expected to grow. Low grade bladder cancer is slow growing. High grade cancer grows more quickly. We want your feedback! Our goal is to provide helpful and easy-to-understand information on cancer. Take our survey to let us know what we got right and what we could do better. NCCN.org/patients/feedback Treatment options for bladder cancer Surgery Intravesical therapy Systemic therapy Radiation therapy Clinical trials Supportive care Side effects of treatment Key points What’s your stage? This chapter explains the different treatment options for bladder cancer. Many people with bladder cancer receive more than one type of treatment. Together, you and your care team will choose a treatment plan that’s right for you. Treatment for bladder cancer is based on many things. This includes the extent, severity, and type of cancer. It also includes your age, your ability to perform daily tasks, your other health issues, and the availability and affordability of drugs. Importantly, treatment is also based on your wishes and preferences. There are several ways to treat bladder cancer. It’s likely that you’ll need a few different types of treatment over months or years. Treatments include: Surgery – The goal of surgery is to take out the tumor and sometimes the surrounding tissue. Surgery procedures range from snipping a single tumor from the bladder lining to removing the entire bladder and other organs. Intravesical therapy – Intravesical therapy involves filling the bladder with a liquid medication that helps destroy cancer cells. Systemic therapy – Systemic therapy is typically an intravenous (IV) treatment that treats cancer anywhere in the whole body. Types of systemic therapy include chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy. Radiation therapy – Radiation therapy is a local therapy, which means it affects a specific area of the body. Sometimes chemotherapy or another therapy is added to radiation therapy to enhance treatment. Clinical trial – Clinical trials are done to test experimental treatments. You may be able to join a clinical trial at any time. Surgery Types of surgery for bladder cancer include tumor removal and bladder removal. Tumor removal For bladder cancer, a surgical procedure called a resection removes the tumor but leaves the rest of the bladder in place. The full name of the procedure is called a transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT, sometimes pronounced tur-bit). Even though it has a complicated name, the concept of TURBT is simple. It’s a procedure that removes tumors through the urethra without having to cut into the abdomen. The tumor can then be tested to provide a more detailed diagnosis. TURBT is done in an operating room in a clinic or hospital. You’ll be given anesthesia to put you to sleep. The urologist then guides an instrument with a small cutting device through the urethra and into the bladder. The instrument is used to examine and remove the tumor from the bladder lining. The goals of TURBT are to: Confirm the initial bladder cancer diagnosis. Take a sample of the bladder wall to see if the tumor has invaded the muscle layer. Remove all the visible tumor. After the procedure, most people can go home the same day. But you’ll need a few days to rest and recover. You may feel pain or burning when you urinate and have blood in your urine for the following 2 weeks or so. Another TURBT procedure may be needed about 2 to 6 weeks after the first one. The second TURBT is done to make sure all the tumor is removed. Or it may be done to get a deeper biopsy sample for the pathologist to examine. A treatment called intravesical therapy is often done in combination with TURBT."], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Non-Muscle Invasive Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of bladder cancer that has not invaded the muscle layer, for which biomarker tests may be used for surveillance.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Chondrosarcoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of bone cancer that starts in cells that form cartilage.<SEP>A type of bone cancer that is typically treated with surgery to remove the tumor.<SEP>A type of bone cancer that may have IDH1 mutations.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A phase of bladder cancer where the cancer has grown on or in the bladder's inner layer of urothelial cells but not into the muscle layer.<SEP>Bladder cancer that has not grown into the muscle layer of the bladder wall, including stages 0 and 1.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Surgery", "entity_type": "method", "description": "Surgery is a broad medical term for procedures that involve physical intervention, typically through an incision, to treat disease, remove cancer or other tissue, or repair the body. In oncology, it is a fundamental treatment modality used to physically remove tumors, cancerous tissue, and sometimes nearby lymph nodes. As a treatment for various cancers, its application and feasibility depend on the cancer type, stage, and location. For instance, it is the most common treatment for basal cell skin cancer and a primary treatment for early-stage cervical cancer, often involving the removal of cancerous tissue. For ovarian cancer, surgery is frequently the initial treatment, often performed by a gynecologic oncologist, and is typically followed by chemotherapy and maintenance therapy; its success depends on tumor size and location. In prostate cancer, surgery involves removing the cancerous tissue, which may include the tumor or the entire prostate, and is an option even for advanced stages. For bladder cancer, the procedure can range from removing the tumor to removing the entire bladder, depending on the stage, and may also involve removing surrounding tissue. In breast cancer, surgery is used as an initial treatment, after which pathologic staging provides more accurate details, and may involve the removal of breast tissue for analysis.\n\nSurgery is also a key treatment for several other malignancies. It is a treatment option for early or locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and for some people with stage 1, 2, or 3 pancreatic cancer to remove cancer from the body. For thyroid cancer, it is a standard treatment involving the physical removal of tissue, with targeted therapy used when surgery cannot be performed. It is a main treatment for glioma, involving tumor removal, and can be the sole treatment for grade 1 gliomas. For chondrosarcoma and other bone cancers, surgery is used to remove tumors. In anal cancer, surgery may be used to remove the anus if the disease does not respond to treatment or returns. However, surgery is not always possible or successful, such as for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), and it is not normally an option for primary CNS lymphoma due to associated risks. Additionally, surgery may have increased side effects for smokers, and staging after the procedure provides more accurate details about the disease.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-168<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682766}, {"entity_name": "Stage 2", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer that has grown into the muscle layer (muscle-invasive) but has not reached the fatty layer or spread elsewhere.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Glioma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A type of brain tumor that arises from glial cells.<SEP>A type of tumor that grows in the brain, developing from glial cells. It is the most common malignant brain tumor in adults and can be challenging to treat.<SEP>A type of tumor that develops from glial cells in the brain or spinal cord. It can disrupt brain function and is often difficult to treat because it invades normal brain tissue.<SEP>A type of brain tumor that can cause vision problems, sensory disturbances, and is linked to blood clots in the legs and lungs. It is identified using imaging tests like MRI.<SEP>A type of tumor that starts in the glial cells of the brain or central nervous system, classified by histology, grade, and biomarkers.<SEP>A type of tumor that arises from glial cells in the brain or spine, graded from 1 to 4 based on aggressiveness.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-168", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683087}, {"entity_name": "Non-Invasive Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Early-stage bladder cancer confined to the inner layer; usually curable.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the bladder, most commonly originating in the urothelial cells that line the inside of the organ. This abnormal cell growth can be classified into several clinical categories, including non-muscle-invasive, muscle-invasive, and metastatic forms, reflecting the extent to which the cancer has spread within or beyond the bladder wall. The disease is most frequently diagnosed in adults aged 55 and older, and its development is influenced by risk factors such as smoking and genetic predisposition.\n\nDiagnosis of bladder cancer typically involves a range of tests and procedures aimed at detecting the presence and extent of the malignancy. The condition is often identified through imaging, urine tests, and biopsy, with the specific diagnostic approach tailored to the individual case. Once diagnosed, the cancer is staged and graded to determine its aggressiveness and spread, which directly informs the treatment strategy.\n\nTreatment for bladder cancer is highly individualized, depending on the stage and grade of the tumor, the patient’s overall health, and their personal preferences. Options may include surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, immunotherapy, or a combination of these approaches. Given the complexity of the disease, management often requires a multidisciplinary care team, including urologists, oncologists, and other specialists, to coordinate comprehensive care. The goal of treatment is to remove or destroy the cancer while preserving bladder function whenever possible, though more advanced or recurrent cases may necessitate more aggressive interventions.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"entity_name": "Stage 2 Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Muscle-invasive bladder cancer where the tumor has invaded the muscle layer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Bladder Removal Surgery", "entity_type": "method", "description": "A surgical procedure to remove the bladder, sometimes offered as an option when BCG treatment is not available or effective.<SEP>A treatment for bladder cancer that can cause side effects such as bleeding, blood clots, kidney problems, infection, sexual dysfunction, incontinence, and problems from urinary diversion.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Chemotherapy", "description": "Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, either directly into the bladder or systemically for metastatic disease.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to kill cancer cells in the body.<SEP>Chemotherapy is used to treat bladder cancer, often before or after surgery, and sometimes as the main treatment for metastatic bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Chemotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer that uses drugs to kill cancer cells throughout the body.", "keywords": "cancer,drugs,option,treatment,treats", "weight": 5.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Chondrosarcoma", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is the usual treatment for chondrosarcoma.", "keywords": "treatment for", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Biopsy", "tgt_id": "Bladder Cancer", "description": "Biopsy is a procedure to analyze a sample of bladder tissue to diagnose bladder cancer.<SEP>Biopsy is used to confirm bladder cancer diagnosis by examining tissue samples.", "keywords": "diagnosis,diagnostic procedure,procedure", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Glioma", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is a main treatment for glioma.<SEP>Surgery is one of the main treatments for glioma.", "keywords": "main treatment,treatment,treatment for", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Radiation Therapy", "description": "Radiation therapy is a common treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Radiation therapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Radiation therapy uses high-energy waves to kill bladder cancer cells and shrink tumors.", "keywords": "high-energy waves,option,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Bladder Removal Surgery", "description": "Bladder removal surgery is a treatment option for bladder cancer, especially in high-risk cases.<SEP>Bladder removal surgery is a treatment for bladder cancer.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment,treatment for", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Clinical Trial", "description": "Clinical trials offer an additional treatment option for bladder cancer patients.<SEP>Clinical trials test experimental treatments for bladder cancer.<SEP>Clinical trials may offer additional treatment options for bladder cancer patients.", "keywords": "offers treatment options,research,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is a common treatment for bladder cancer, used alone or with other therapies.<SEP>Surgery is a treatment option for bladder cancer.", "keywords": "option,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "CT Scan", "description": "CT scan is used to detect bladder cancer spread.", "keywords": "detects", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Pancreatic Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is a treatment option for some people with stage 1, 2, or 3 pancreatic cancer.", "keywords": "option,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "MRI", "description": "MRI is used to get detailed views of bladder cancer and check for spread.", "keywords": "detects", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Surgery", "tgt_id": "Treatment Decision", "description": "Surgery is one of the treatment options considered in decision-making.", "keywords": "consideration,option", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Immunotherapy", "description": "Immunotherapy is a treatment option for bladder cancer, sometimes delivered directly into the bladder.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a systemic therapy that uses the immune system to treat bladder cancer.<SEP>Immunotherapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.", "keywords": "cancer,immune system,option,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Treatment", "description": "Treatment for prostate cancer depends on the risk group and may include surgery or radiation therapy.", "keywords": "management,therapy", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Physical Exam", "description": "Physical exam may detect signs of bladder cancer by feeling for abnormalities.", "keywords": "detection,diagnosis", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is a treatment option for some people with early or locally advanced NSCLC.", "keywords": "cancer,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Pathologist", "description": "The pathologist examines bladder cancer cells to determine the grade by comparing them to normal cells.", "keywords": "diagnosis,grading", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Prostate cancer can be treated with surgery.<SEP>Surgery is a treatment option for prostate cancer, often used for higher-risk cases.<SEP>Surgery is a treatment option for advanced-stage prostate cancer.", "keywords": "management,treated by,treatment", "weight": 3.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Cervical cancer", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Surgery is often the main treatment for early-stage cervical cancer.", "keywords": "treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683104}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Family History", "description": "Having a family history of bladder cancer increases risk.", "keywords": "risk factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Chapter 5", "tgt_id": "Surgery", "description": "Chapter 5 explains treatment with surgery for early or locally advanced NSCLC.", "keywords": "guideline,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Tumor", "description": "Bladder cancer may form a tumor in the bladder.", "keywords": "pathology,progression", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Radiation Therapy", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Systemic therapy may be used in combination with radiation therapy to treat CSCC that can't be removed using surgery.<SEP>Systemic therapy may be used in addition to radiation therapy in some cases of high-risk CSCC.", "keywords": "combination,combined with,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Systemic Therapy", "description": "Systemic therapy is a treatment for bladder cancer.<SEP>Systemic therapy is used to treat bladder cancer by affecting the whole body.", "keywords": "treatment,treats", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Curettage and Electrodesiccation", "tgt_id": "Low-risk CSCC", "description": "Curettage and electrodesiccation is a recommended surgical treatment for low-risk, local CSCC.", "keywords": "surgery,treatment", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683097}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Targeted Therapy", "description": "Targeted therapy is a treatment option for bladder cancer.<SEP>Targeted therapy is used for bladder cancer, especially for people with specific gene mutations.", "keywords": "gene mutations,option,treatment", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Pr